\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  foreign reserves fall for third month after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par 07:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at $1.2 trln, 2nd largest after China\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rises in U.S. bond yields overwhelm other factors\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 MOF record shows no stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen unsustainable given U.S. ties\par \par \uc2 (Adds graphic)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 8 (Reuters) - Japan's foreign reserves extended declines in October, following the previous month's record drop, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday, reflecting the largest ever amount of yen-buying, dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The data comes alongside separate figures that confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not conduct stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September and only entered the market to buy yen for U.S. dollars on Sept. 22 as announced, its first foray into the market to prop up the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Market players are scrutinising \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vast pool of foreign assets and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  records for clues on how much more \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be willing to spend in its forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, though authorities remain tight-lipped on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't be surprised if authorities conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  one more time if dollar gains accelerate to hit new high beyond 152 yen," said Tohru Sasaki, head of Japan Markets Research at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "But I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can continue large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling U.S. Treasuries to buy the yen indefinitely. There should be a limit given relations with its ally the United States."\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have said they keep in close contact with U.S. counterparts, brushing aside speculation of U.S. opposition to dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while the United States is raising interest rates to battle inflation.\par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves fell for a third consecutive month to $1.19 trillion as at the end of October, still the world's second-largest after China, the ministry said. The decline of $43.5 billion marked the second sharpest month-on-month on record.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rising foreign bond yields more than offset other factors that would support reserves, such as higher valuations of other foreign assets and income gains from foreign bond holdings, officials said.\par \par \uc2 By asset type, foreign bonds - most of which are widely believed to be U.S. Treasuries, bought during bouts of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past when the yen was strong - account for four-fifths of the reserves.\par \par \uc2 Of the reserves, foreign bonds fell the most in September and suffered the second-largest drop last month to $941 billion. Officials would not elaborate but market watchers interpret the drops as reflecting the sale of U.S. Treasuries for yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Deposits - mostly parked at overseas central banks and the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 , which make up some one-tenth of the reserves and can be readily converted into cash - grew for a second consecutive month to $137 billion in October.\par \par \uc2 Separate data on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which includes quarterly and daily totals, confirmed that authorities did not conduct stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September, having spent 2.8 trillion yen that month to support the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a record 6.35 trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month as the yen hit a 32-year low near 152 yen to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yen has remained under pressure as the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  remains committed to keeping ultra-low interest rates, in sharp contrast to aggressive rate hikes by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 . ($1 = 146.8700 yen)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Sam Holmes and Christopher Cushing) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-8T07:10:44.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221108eib8008y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
