'20\'22 \uc2 Canada's government\uc2  tosses fiscal hot potato to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 07:00 GB Oct Halifax HPI MM -0.1% prev; YY 9.90% prev \u8226\'20\'22 07:00 DE Sept Industrial Output MM 0.2% f'cast, -0.8% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 EZ Nov Sentix Index -35.0 f'cast, -38.3 prev Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 N/A ECB Panetta, Enria at Brussels Eurogroup meeting \u8226\'20\'22 08:40 ECB Lagarde pre-recorded speech for Frankfurt conference \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 BoE Breeden speaks at London ISDA-AIMI conference \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 ECB Panetta in Frankfurt panel discussion \u8226\'20\'22 17:00 BoE Pill in virtual Q&A Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY range-bound, tight range, JPY crosses buoyant \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY was stuck in a 146.71-147.35 EBS range in Asia, market more settled \u8226\'20\'22 Specs seem less bullish as of Nov 1, IMM CTA JPY shorts off large \u8226\'20\'22 Talk again too of eventual Fed pivot, helping to cap US yields for now \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese importers still ready to buy dips, exporters to sell rallies \u8226\'20\'22 Option expiries to have little impact till Thursday, today only 148 handles \u8226\'20\'22 Asia more risk on today, Nikkei +1.2% @27,528, E-Minis -0.2% @3772 \u8226\'20\'22 JPY crosses looking buoyant, EUR/JPY 145.80 to 146.29 EBS \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/JPY 165.63 to 166.79 after moves down last week, AUD/JPY 93.82 to 94.69 EUR/USD-Recovers most of early losses but still down on the day \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD fell to 0.9875 early Asia after closing +2.15% at 0.9959 Friday \u8226\'20\'22 USD broadly gained due to China pushing back on rumours they were ready to ease Covid restrictions \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD steadily climbed back after risk assets in Asia took the China news in stride \u8226\'20\'22 AXJ equity index rose 1.5% and E-minis eased just 0.15% after opening -0.75% \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon the EUR/USD is trading 0.9940/45 \u8226\'20\'22 The 10-day MA is at 0.9920 with support at the 21-day MA at 0.9848 \u8226\'20\'22 Key resistance is at the 100-day MA at 1.0046 with sellers 0.9970/80 \u8226\'20\'22 Key will be moves in US yields and risk assets ahead of Thursday's US CPI AUD/USD-Eases in Asia after China vowed to maintain zero Covid policy \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD gapped down to 0.6401 early Asia after closing 0.6470 Friday \u8226\'20\'22 Pair gained 2.89% Friday in part due to rumours China would ease zero Covid policy \u8226\'20\'22 On Saturday China vowed to maintain the policy and led to AUD/USD gap lower \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD filled some of the gap to rebound to 0.6449 before sellers capped \u8226\'20\'22 Heading onto the afternoon the AUD/USD is trading around 0.6420 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD tracked CNH- as it eased 0.70% following Friday's 2.0% rise \u8226\'20\'22 Asian markets remained steady despite the China news \u8226\'20\'22 The \uc2 Hang Seng\uc2  added another 2.0% and the AXJ index was up 1.0% \u8226\'20\'22 E-minis opened 0.75% lower but pared losses to 0.20% late morning \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD support is at the 10-day MA at 0.6406 and 21-day MA at 0.6346 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the Oct 22 high at 0.6522 GBP/USD-China-led gap lower at the open, then pared losses \u8226\'20\'22 Off 0.4% in a busy 1.1300-1.1343 range - opened lower as USD gapped higher \u8226\'20\'22 USD bid, risk sold at the open on China to maintain it's zero-COVID policy \u8226\'20\'22 UK nurses begin biggest strike ballot in more than 100 years \u8226\'20\'22 Six in ten Britons to spend less at Christmas, tough UK winter \u8226\'20\'22 Charts, daily momentum studies 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages conflict \u8226\'20\'22 21 day Bollinger bands contract - charts return to a neutral bias \u8226\'20\'22 1.1300 Asian low and 1.1371 NY close Friday initial support, resistance \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling to remain volatile as diverse elements collide USD/JPY choppy but in core 145-150 range for now USD/JPY has experienced some volatility since the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in earnest to slow or even halt further yen weakness on Oct 21. Yet the pair has mostly held between 145-150, which looks to remain the core range with many expecting fresh Japanese action on rallies towards 150. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been confirmed at 6.35 trillion yen in October and 2.8 trillion in September. Some players in Tokyo suggest there may have been more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into November, notably on Nov 1-2 after USD/JPY rebounded towards 149.00. Although there will not be any confirmation until December when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  releases fresh data, Japanese officials have made it clear they would like to see JPY weakness contained at present. Even \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  chimed in, threatening possible future tweaks to yield curve control, a major factor behind JPY weakness as interest rate differentials with other major economies widen significantly. A move may even be possible before Kuroda's term ends in April 2023. The latest IMM CFTC data suggest speculators have digested these developments. JPY shorts dropped dramatically in the week ended Nov 1 and could fall further given the current stalemate between Japanese officials and the market. The wild card is where U.S. yields go next. With the debate over a Federal Reserve pivot persisting, rises in longer-term Treasury yields could be capped ahead of their late October highs. Sterling to remain volatile as diverse elements collide Sterling is likely to remain volatile as a swathe of domestic and international factors collide. The pound fell 2% last Thursday after dovish \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  comments, bounced 1.9% Friday on hopes the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  may reduce the pace of rate hikes, and fell 0.5% early in Asia Monday, as the U.S. dollar climbed on China reaffirming it's zero-COVID policy. U.S. midterm elections this week could easily provoke further market uncertainty and turbulence. In the UK, the Nov 17 autumn statement from \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 's government will be pivotal for sterling. The Guardian reports that up to 60 billion pounds of tax rises and spending cuts may be announced. If markets perceive the package strikes a credible balance to navigate inflation and recession pressures, sterling should climb. If the measures to tackle the budget deficit deepen recession fears, sterling will fall. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlooks and yield spreads currently weigh on GBP/USD. The \uc2 BoE\uc2  said last week that market rate-hike pricing was too aggressive, as prolonged recession fears mount. This contrasted with the Fed, which expects yields to rise higher than forecast, though it may slow the pace of hikes. Technically the daily charts show no strong bias, with GBP/USD near the midpoint of a choppy 1.0925-1.1645 range, in place since late September. A close outside this range should extend. Week Ahead-U.S. midterm elections, China COVID policy lead The U.S. midterm elections take place Tuesday with the Republicans expected to win the majority in the House of Representatives, while the Senate is a close call. Republican control of Congress with a Democrat in the White House is seen by many analysts as market-friendly. But there is a danger the Republicans could balk at raising the debt ceiling as leverage to pass some of their spending-cut initiatives. Markets rallied hard on Friday in anticipation China would start lifting some of its COVID restrictions, but weekend reports suggest Chinese officials are happy with the current approach and not ready to relax it anytime soon. Markets will be looking for greater clarity on Beijing's policy in the week ahead. Week Ahead-U.S. CPI will be key for Fed expectations Federal Reserve expectations swung back and forth last week, triggering some violent market moves. U.S. consumer inflation data, due Thursday, is likely to determine expectations for whether the Fed will again raise rates by 75 basis points in December, or scale back to 50 bps. Other important U.S. data in the week ahead includes weekly jobless claims and \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  preliminary November consumer sentiment. Key data in Europe includes German industrial production and inflation, and euro zone retail sales. The UK has IP, September and flash Q3 GDP data, and the trade balance. China's October data will be in focus after Friday's big gains in the yuan and Chinese equities. Trade data kicked off the week, with export and import growth both slowing more than expected from September. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data will be closely eyed following bouts of rumoured \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Chinese CPI and PPI are also due, and credit data may be released too. Japan has current account and trade data, along with household spending. In Australia, the \uc2 NAB\uc2  business survey and consumer sentiment are on tap. New Zealand and Canada have no top-tier data due.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-10T05:44:29.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221107eib700r31\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
