\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID-Attention  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par 22:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nov 7 (Reuters) - A look at the day ahead in Asian markets from Jamie McGeever. Figures from Beijing on Monday showed that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves unexpectedly rose in October, but there are unlikely to be similar surprises in Japan's numbers due early on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 They will almost certainly show a decline in official reserves, with investors hoping the data sheds light on exactly where Tokyo's record $42.8 billion yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month came from.\par \par \uc2 China's official stash rose $23.47 billion last month to $3.052 trillion - analysts had expected a fall to $3.018 trillion - while Japan held $1.24 trillion at the end of September.\par \par \uc2 China's increase may be down to the euro's rise of almost 1% against the dollar last month. A fairly large chunk of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are thought to be in euro-denominated assets, unlike Japan's, which are overwhelmingly invested in dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $42.8 billion last month to prop up the yen. This followed almost $20 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September.\par \par \uc2 China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are the world's top two \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves holders, and how they manage their $4 trillion of reserves has huge ripple effects for world markets, especially U.S. Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 If either or both sell Treasuries outright to support their exchange rates, U.S. yields could spike up, tightening U.S. and global financial conditions. There are already growing concerns surrounding U.S. bond market liquidity without two of the world's largest holders of Treasuries actively selling.\par \par \uc2 Around 10% of Japan's reserves are held as deposits parked with foreign official institutions and can be readily tapped for dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Maybe Tokyo dipped into this pool of funding before directly selling Treasuries.\par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves fell a record $54 billion in September and in percentage terms, the 4.2% decline was the biggest since 1998. That was in large part due to valuation effects though - some form of dollar asset sales will figure more prominently in October's report.\par \par \uc2 Three key developments that could provide more direction to markets on Tuesday:\par \par \uc2 Japan household spending (September)\par \par \uc2 Australia consumer sentiment (November)\par \par \uc2 India trade balance (October)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jamie McGeever in Orlando, Fla.; Editing by Josie Kao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-7T22:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221107eib7043at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
