\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Why Japan is the only major economy to maintain negative interest rates and the implications for the country\b0\par\par\uc2 1,319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 While the U.S. has been hiking interest rates at full speed this year to control high inflation, the world's major central banks have embarked on a similar crusade. Except one: Japan.\par \par \uc2 Swimming against the tide, the world's third-largest economy has maintained a -0.1% interest rate, even though the country hit an inflationary level of 3%, the highest since 1991 (excluding the 2014 jump, when prices were hit by a sales tax hike).\par \par \uc2 In many countries around the world, 3% inflation would be minimal (just ask Argentina, which is currently dealing with an 83% cost-of-living increase).\par \par \uc2 But by Japan's standards, this is the highest inflation in decades.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen, is in free fall.\par \par \uc2 5 keys to understanding why China's economy is in troubleWhy Japan's inflation wave sweeping the world is good news for JapanNorth Korea tests one of its missiles over Japan, forcing residents of Hokkaido island to take shelterWho was \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 , the "hawk" who brought political stability to Japan and created "Abenomics"?Conventional economic prescriptions say that a rise in interest rates could alleviate both problems. However, many are not convinced that this is the solution for the Asian country.\par \par \uc2 With much of the current inflationary pressure coming from the strong dollar and rising energy products and imported food, some economists believe that the Japanese authorities' room for maneuver is, for now, quite limited.\par \par \uc2 GETTY IMAGESY hikemay partially help the country move closer to its 2% annual target, but what is not clear is that it will halt the yen's slide\par \par \uc2 .In other countries, rate hikes by their central banks have done little to protect their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the mighty dollar at this juncture.\par \par \uc2 What is a negative interest rate used for?\par \par \uc2 Negative interest rates seek to revive the economy.\par \par \uc2 In practice, it means that a country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , charges commercial banks for some of the reserves they hold at the institution.\par \par \uc2 GETTY IMAGESinceit is more costly for commercial banks to hold reserves, the measure is designed to encourage banks to use those reserves to lend to entrepreneurs in an effort to revive economic activity\par \par \uc2 .The Bank of Japan\uc2  argues that making lending more expensive at this time would only suppress already weak demand and delay a fragile post-pandemic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "Good"\par \par \uc2 inflationJapan's history has its own twists and turns that set it apart from the rest\par \par \uc2 .For years the country was mired in distressing deflation (a generalized decline in prices) that contributed to economic stagnation.\par \par \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , governor of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , insists the economy is too weak to raise rates. GETTY IMAGESPreciselywhat Japan needed was a rise in the cost of living to stimulate economic growth. However, analysts say, today's inflation in Japan is not a reflection of a real recovery, but more the result of the external crisis and the fall of the yen.\par \par \uc2 From this point of view, what Japan would need is "good inflation", of the kind created by strong consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 But the current one is a kind of "bad inflation," experts say, of the kind created by a strong dollar and pandemic- and war-related commodity shortages in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considers this price increase to be temporary and therefore prefers to leave rates below zero.\par \par \uc2 That is why the top \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , has insisted that the economy is too weak to manage higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The yen's plummeting valueTraditionally,\par \par \uc2 the yen has always been seen as a safe haven in times of storm and so whenever there is a crisis, investors protect themselves by buying the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 .But that status is now in question.\par \par \uc2 This year alone, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost a fifth of its value relative to the dollar, reaching its lowest price since 1990.\par \par \uc2 This year alone, the yen has lost a fifth of its value relative to the dollar. GETTY IMAGESWhy? The yen's fall has been driven by the difference between interest rates in Japan and the US, explains BBC journalist Mariko Oi.\par \par \uc2 While the world's leading economy has aggressively raised its interest rate to 3.25%, Japan has remained below zero.\par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates tend to make a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to investors.\par \par \uc2 As a result, there is less demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from countries with lower rates and those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lose value.\par \par \uc2 Some experts believe the yen's weakness also reflects the state of the country's finances, with an economy that has barely grown over the past three decades.\par \par \uc2 Add to that the fact that Japan is the world's most indebted country and carries a demographic time bomb due to a low birth rate.\par \par \uc2 The government has allowed some foreign workers to help address the problem of an aging population, but there is still strong opposition to immigration policies.\par \par \uc2 "There is no reason for the yen to strengthen," Takeshi Fujimaki, a former adviser to billionaire investor \uc2 George Soros\uc2 , tells the BBC.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the yen for nearly two and a half decades.\par \par \uc2 However, last month, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell, the authorities stepped in and spent US$21 billion. The measure helped for a short time, but soon the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell again, this time crossing 150 yen to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Some reports say it had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, says Mariko Oi, although the Japanese government denies this.\par \par \uc2 In any case, local analysts believe that this type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only have a one-off effect.\par \par \uc2 "It is to show the Japanese government's position that it does not want a further weakening of the Japanese yen," says Eisuke Sakakibara, a former senior official at \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The umaibo variableAny\par \par \uc2 country whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loses value faces adverse effects. The weak yen has made everything Japan buys abroad more expensive.\par \par \uc2 The country is heavily dependent on imported oil and gas. Due to exchange rates and rising energy prices, the amount of money it spent on imports last month increased by 46%.\par \par \uc2 But on the positive side, the money Japanese exporters earn abroad is worth much more at home. Given that exports account for about 15% of the country's total economic activity, it's a boon amid the bad news.\par \par \uc2 From a consumer standpoint, the Japanese have lost half their purchasing power over the past decade, triggering a serious problem because average wages in Japan have barely increased in more than three decades.\par \par \uc2 GETTY IMAGESThecurrent inflation, although only reaching 3% compared to other developed countries that have levels above 10% per year, is something the Japanese were not used to\par \par \uc2 .Great surprise was caused by the 20% increase of one of the most common snacks in Japan, the umaibo, a product that has always been priced at 10 yen (US$0.075) since its creation 43 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Such was the impact that Yaokin, the company that makes the popular snack, had to launch an advertising campaign explaining why it was forced to raise the price.\par \par \uc2 As was inevitable, one by one the products have gone up in value. According to the Teikoku data bank, the prices of more than 10,000 food products will increase by an average of 13% this year.\par \par \uc2 "Consumers are not used to accepting inflation," \uc2 Takeshi Niinami\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 Suntory Holdings\uc2 , a company known for Japanese whiskies Yamazaki, Hibiki and Hakushu, as well as beer and soft drinks such as bottled water and coffee, tells the BBC.\par \par \uc2 That discontent is a challenge that will have to be faced by the government of Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2 , who has so far fully supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decisions to hold interest rates. (I)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221105eib50085f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
