 worse than 0.4% drop f/c \u8226\'20\'22 Exports also fell 0.5% mm when f/c rising 0.1% \u8226\'20\'22 Data suggest a significant slowdown followed first \uc2 ECB\uc2  75bps hike \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2  just repeated that huge interest rate hike \u8226\'20\'22 A lot of cash has recently been invested in EUR Dollar down versus yen, \uc2 BOJ\uc2  and MOF "fill the airwaves" \u8226\'20\'22 Dollar sags as Fed decision looms; yen surges \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY has dropped from 148.35 to 147.03, on Wed, according to EBS prices \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY chart bullish, on balance, as momentum remains positive \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Kuroda: recent yen weakening negative for Japan's economy \u8226\'20\'22 Fin Min Suzuki: ready to deal immediately in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \u8226\'20\'22 But yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will merely allow USD/JPY bulls to buy it cheaper \u8226\'20\'22 Kuroda hints at tweak to ultra-low rates as future option \u8226\'20\'22 Euro to climb against yen in November, as usual GBP/USD is no longer oversold - that's key \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD isn't oversold on daily, weekly or monthly charts \u8226\'20\'22 Sep-Oct steeply oversold conditions resulted in a rebound \u8226\'20\'22 Rebound briefly led to overbought situation on daily chart \u8226\'20\'22 Thursday event risk - BOE 75bps hike, Oct PMI seen unchanged at 47.5 \u8226\'20\'22 Influential levels: Sep high 1.1738, 100-DMA 1.1717 and 55-DMA 1.1405 \u8226\'20\'22 Daily Ichimoku cloud top 1.1309 and 21-DMA 1.1317 \u8226\'20\'22 Continuation of downtrend towards 1.0737, 1.0175 and 0.9721 targets likely AUD/USD attempting to negate Tuesday slide \u8226\'20\'22 Aussie trying to buck a three-day decline from 0.6522 Oct. 27 high \u8226\'20\'22 Softer U.S. yields helping underpin ahead of today's Fed rate news \u8226\'20\'22 Frothy commodity prices also supporting the AUD: copper a stand out gainer \u8226\'20\'22 A less aggressive \uc2 RBA\uc2  stance will drag on price: economic uncertainty cited \u8226\'20\'22 Support at 0.6396, 10DMA: briefly breached but intact on a closing basis \u8226\'20\'22 Bigger level at 0.6368, Oct. 31 low \u8226\'20\'22 Topside and initial resistance at 0.6464, Nov. 1 failure high \u8226\'20\'22 Resumption of recent bull run on a break above 0.6522, Oct. 27 high Euro to climb against yen in November, as usual History shows that the euro usually climbs against the Japanese yen in November, this trend will likely continue this year. A study of EUR/JPY's seasonal performance for each November since 2000 shows it has posted a positive return in 15 of the last 22 years, highlighting a bullish bias. Seasonality should not be considered in isolation, but when combined with other factors it can be a useful tool. The Bank of Japan raised its inflation forecasts last week but maintained ultra-low interest rates, remaining a dovish outlier among a global wave of central banks tightening monetary policy. By contrast the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates for the third meeting in a row and signalled an intention to fight record-high inflation. The medium-term technical outlook is bullish. EUR/JPY's 14-week momentum reading remains positive, reinforcing the overall scope for a probe of the 2014 149.79 (EBS) peak and 150.00 psychological level just above. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3sShwdi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3WmOHDu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Norway's crown faces a tough time through November The charts currently suggest that the NOK could extend its recovery from its 10.5050 Oct. 21 low versus the euro but fundamentally the odds favour weakness. The Norwegian crown has strengthened versus the euro since late September and has recouped around 40% of its August to September drop. But potential for a slight change in \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  policy could trigger a reversal in the NOK's fortunes. The \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  meets this Thursday, and the market is split between a hike of 25-basis points and a half-point move. A smaller hike outcome would therefore have negative implications for the NOK. A larger move is likely to be overshadowed by Wednesday's widely expected 75-basis point hike from the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 . The \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  has also announced that it will be selling NOK 3.7 billion per-day through November, down on the October amount but still likely to weigh heavily on the crown. Analysts point to the net flow from oil and gas companies along with the \uc2 Norges Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action leaning on the NOK through the remainder off the year. EUR/NOK has key support at 10.1886, daily Ichimoku cloud top and 10.1684, the 100 day moving average line. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3SXloUY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-5T10:37:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221102eib2029mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
