\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: Japan Supported  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  With $42.8 Billion in Funds -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megumi Fujikawa \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par 07:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -- Japan spent more than $40 billion in October to prop up the yen, an effort that has had some success but remains hampered by Washington's lack of interest in stemming the dollar's rise.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the government spent Yen6.35 trillion in October on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , equivalent to $42.8 billion. The figure came on top of the Yen2.8 trillion it spent in September.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said on Sept. 22 that the government was buying yen to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling, the first such action in 24 years. Since then, the government has declined to confirm individual actions, but Mr. Suzuki has repeatedly implied that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was continuing by saying he wouldn't tolerate speculative moves in the yen.\par \par \uc2 The release of the October report on Monday confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place during the month.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is part of broader global concern about the strength of the dollar this year, driven by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's push to raise interest rates rapidly, which makes dollar-denominated assets look more attractive to investors.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been particularly hard hit because the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is keeping interest rates near zero. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 32-year intraday low of 151.93 yen to the dollar on Oct. 21, according to data provider Quick.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including two instances that likely followed the Oct. 21 fall, has been successful in putting a floor on the yen. On Monday evening in Tokyo, a dollar bought 148.57 yen, and the dollar has remained below 150 during the past week.\par \par \uc2 However, analysts say a significant reversal of the yen's weakness this year is unlikely because the interest-rate gap between the U.S. and Japan is still widening.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  kept its interest-rate targets at ultralow levels despite upward revisions in its inflation forecasts, saying wage growth was still sluggish and the economy needed the support of low interest rates. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is widely expected to raise rates by an additional 0.75 percentage point this week.\par \par \uc2 Izuru Kato, president of Totan Research, said the yen could have fallen to 160 to the dollar without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but he said there was a contradiction between the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort to prop up the yen and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easing policy dating back nearly a decade.\par \par \uc2 The policy, "which includes negative interest rates and fixing the 10-year [government-bond] yield at 0.25%, was in practice aimed at weakening the yen," Mr. Kato said. "If the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  keeps the policy, the trend wouldn't change even if the MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves totaled $1.12 trillion as of Sept. 30, giving it more room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but at the pace of more than $60 billion in two months -- the amount for September and October combined -- it would eventually run low.\par \par \uc2 That is where some cooperation from the U.S. would help. Recent comments by President Biden and Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  have made clear that is unlikely.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not concerned about the strength of the dollar. I'm concerned about the rest of the world," Mr. Biden said during a campaign stop in Portland, Ore., on Oct. 15. "The problem is the lack of economic growth and sound policy in other countries, not so much ours."\par \par \uc2 In the U.S. where inflation remains at 8%, a strong dollar has eased the burden of American consumers by reducing the cost of imports. Ms. Yellen has said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are best left to markets to decide.\par \par \uc2 Japan has long disclosed each month how much it spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For 129 consecutive months -- since it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2011 to push down the yen -- the figure was zero. Now each month's disclosure is watched closely by market participants.\par \par \uc2 Write to Megumi Fujikawa at megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (November 1, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 01, 2022 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221101eib1000jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
