 30, 2022 : 19,459,119 As of March 31, 2022 : 19,470,556\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3. Average number of outstanding shares during the periods:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the first half ended September 30, 2022 (accumulative): 230,832,587\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the first half ended September 30, 2021 (accumulative): 236,057,609\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note1: The Company conducted a five-for-one share consolidation of shares of common stock effective October 1, 2021. Average number of issued shares during the periods for the first half ended September 30, 2021 (accumulative) have been calculated based on the assumption that the share consolidation had been conducted on April 1, 2021.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note2: The Company established the Executive Compensation Board Incentive Plan Trust. The trust account\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 953,699 stocks in the second quarter ended September 30, 2022 and 1,003,203 stocks in the financial year ended March 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * This summary of consolidated financial results is not subject to quarterly reviews.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Important Note Concerning the Appropriate Use of Business Forecasts and other\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This document contains forward-looking statements based on information available to the company at the time of disclosure and certain assumptions that management believes to be reasonable. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sojitz\f28 \uc2 \f2  makes no assurances as to the actual results and/or other outcomes, which may differ substantially from those expressed or implied by forward- looking statements due to various factors including changes in economic conditions in key markets, both in and outside of Japan, and exchange rate movements. The Company will provide timely disclosure of any material changes, events, or other relevant issues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supplementary materials on financial results can be found on the Company's web site. The company will hold an IR meeting on financial results for the first half ended September 30, 2022 for analysts and institutional investors on November 2, 2022. Contents (materials and videos) of the meeting and condensed transcript of Q&A session will be posted on the company's web site immediately after the meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1. Analysis of Business Results\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (1) Overview of the Six-Month Period Ended September 30, 2022\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic Environment\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the six-month period ended September 30, 2022, increasingly strong downward pressure was placed on the global economy by factors including Russia's military invasion of Ukraine, the economic sanctions placed on Russia in response to this act, high inflation, monetary tightening measures instituted on a global scale to address inflation, and the opaque outlook for the Chinese economy. Accordingly, there is a need to carefully monitor factors with the potential to trigger future economic recession, despite the dissipation of the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the United States, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Board\f28 \uc2 \f2  continues to implement large interest rate hikes, and the latest projections, issued at the September 2022 Federal Open Market Committee meeting, forecast a further increase of 1.25 percentage points to be instituted at the two remaining meetings to be held within the year. Moreover, housing and capital investment is slowing, creating concern for a potential recession leading up to 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Europe, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has put forth a policy of aggressive interest rate hikes to stabilize prices, which it has defined as a priority issue. As for energy, European countries are instituting increasingly harsh sanctions on Russia. At the same time, however, these countries are also highly dependent on crude oil and gas produced in Russia, a situation that is expected to have an adverse impact on the value chains of the manufacturing industry and a wide range of other industries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's zero-COVID strategy placed great pressure on economic activities during the first quarter. Economic activities were resumed in the second quarter, but the economy continued to be weak, with real GDP growth of only 3.9% year on year in the period from July to September 2022 and no consistent improvements seen in the Purchasing Managers' Index or in other economic indicators. Moreover, caution is required with regard to the economic figures to be announced going forward and trends related to the ongoing implementation of the zero-COVID strategy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Asia, production and exports continue to recover in conjunction with the resumption of economic activities. At the same time, however, the central banks of Asian countries began raising interest rates in May 2022 to combat the growing costs of imported goods stemming from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation and the capital outfluxes resulted from interest rate hikes in the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Production and exports in Japan were heavily impacted by the lockdowns instituted in China during the first quarter, but a recovery trend has continued since second quarter. However, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  has decided that it will continue its prior monetary easing measures, even amid the global monetary tightening trend. The ability of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is limited. Accordingly, caution is warranted with regard to the potential economic impacts of yen depreciation resulted from the further widening of the gap in interest rates between the United States and Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial Performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sojitz Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2 's consolidated business results for the six-month period ended September 30, 2022 are presented below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue               Revenue was up 27.2% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A51,273,322 million, due to higher\par                       revenue in the Metals, Mineral Resources & Recycling Division, a result of higher\par                       prices for coal; in the Chemicals Division, a result of growth in plastic resin\par                       transactions and higher methanol prices; and in the Consumer Industry &\par                       Agriculture Business, a result of higher prices for lumber and fertilizer.\par Gross profit          Gross profit was up \u165\'00\'A564,527 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A5182,243 million, due to\par                       higher profit in the Metals, Mineral Resources & Recycling Division, a result of\par                       higher prices for coal; in the Chemicals Division, a result of higher methanol prices\par                       and growth in plastic resin transactions; and in the Automotive Division, a result\par                       of improvement in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate and profitability in overseas automobile\par                       \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par Profit before tax     Profit before tax was up \u165\'00\'A554,092 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A5106,547 million, as a\par                       result of increases in gross profit, despite an increase in selling, general and\par                       administrative expenses associated with the acquisition of new consolidated\par                       subsidiaries.\par Profit for the period After deducting income tax expenses of \u165\'00\'A524,936 million from profit before tax of\par                       \u165\'00\'A5106,547 million, profit for the period amounted to \u165\'00\'A581,611 million, up \u165\'00\'A540,147\par                       million year on year. Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company)\par                       increased \u165\'00\'A539,427 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A578,876 million.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comprehensive Comprehensive income for the period of \u165\'00\'A5156,690 million was recorded, up income for the period \u165\'00\'A595,991 million year on year, following an increase in financial assets at fair\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 value through other comprehensive income along with more beneficial foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  translation differences for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Comprehensive income for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) was \u165\'00\'A5148,583 million, an increase of \u165\'00\'A590,236 million year on year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                              (In Millions of Yen)\par                                                                 \par                           Fiscal Year Fiscal Year Difference   Percentage\par                           2022 H1     2021 H1                change\par                           (A)-(B)      \par                           Results(A)  Results (B)            (%)\par                                        \par Revenue                   1,273,322   1,000,694   272,628      27.2\par Gross profit              182,243     117,716     64,527       54.8\par Profit before tax         106,547     52,455      54,092       103.1\par Profit for the period     81,611      41,464      40,147       96.8\par Profit for the period                                           \par attributable to owners of 78,876      39,449      39,427       99.9\par the Company                                                     \par Comprehensive income      156,690     60,699      95,991       158.1\par for the period             \par                                                               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Results for the six-month period ended September 30, 2022, are summarized by segment below. Effective April 1, 2022, the Company reorganized its Consumer Industry & Agriculture Business and Retail & Consumer Service segments and changed its reporting segment classification method.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Automotive\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue was up 27.0% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A5151,185 million, due to improvement in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate and profitability in overseas automobile \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) rose \u165\'00\'A51,051 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A54,482 million, as a result of an increase in gross profit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aerospace & Transportation Project\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue was down 40.5% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A518,709 million, due to the lower revenue in aircraft-related transactions. Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) increased \u165\'00\'A5617 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A52,001 million, as a result of an increase in gross profit, due to the acquisition of in-flight catering business.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Infrastructure & Healthcare\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue was up 68.3% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A547,311 million, due to the acquisition of energy conservation business in the U.S. Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) increased \u165\'00\'A57,744 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A58,981 million, due to an increase in gross profit, share of profit of investments accounted for using the equity method in LNG businesses and in other income (expenses) by a partial sale of an overseas telecommunications tower operating company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Metals, Mineral Resources & Recycling\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue was up 35.2% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A5349,881 million, due to higher prices for coal. Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) increased \u165\'00\'A525,580 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A541,794 million, as a result of an increase in gross profit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chemicals\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue was up 24.1% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A5323,479 million, due to growth in plastic resin transactions and higher methanol prices. Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) increased \u165\'00\'A54,583 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A511,340 million, as a result of an increase in gross profit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer Industry & Agriculture Business\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue was up 35.8% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A5192,632 million, due to higher prices in lumber and fertilizer. Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) increased \u165\'00\'A51,142 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A55,887 million, as a result of an increase in gross profit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retail & Consumer Service\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue was up 45.4% year on year, to \u165\'00\'A5149,444 million, due to the acquisition of aquaculture food product manufacturing and processing company. Profit for the period (attributable to owners of the Company) decreased \u165\'00\'A5479 million year on year, to \u165\'00\'A51,418 million, despite an increase in gross profit, as a result of an increase of selling, general and administrative expenses, due to the acquisition of aquaculture food product manufacturing and processing company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is an excerpt of the original content. To continue reading it, access the original document \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.publicnow.com