\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-BREAKINGVIEWS-Bank of Japan fiddles while bond market burns\b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2022\par 14:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Oct 31 (Reuters Breakingviews) - \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  is playing with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  fire. The yield-curve control policy in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempts to hold down interest rates by buying \u8211\'20\'13 or threatening to buy - unlimited amounts of sovereign bonds, is breaking down and risks freezing the market over, a bad look for an international reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kuroda\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to put a floor under the plunging yen, which at nearly 150 per U.S. dollar is at its weakest since 1990, appear to be losing traction. He could make tweaks to the bank\u8217\'20\'19s unorthodox monetary policy, but they might not accomplish much given fundamentals. The root of the issue is the profound gap between American and Japanese economic conditions.\par \par \uc2 With consumer costs spiking in Western economies, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been hiking borrowing rates, but in Japan inflation remains relatively tepid and growth anemic. September economic data for Japan was grim, with the import bill spiking and factory activity slowing. Credit austerity could profoundly inhibit economic activity.\par \par \uc2 Annual consumer inflation, at 3%, is subdued by global standards, but it is not the kind of demand- and wage-driven price rises the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has been trying to deliver. Instead, it reflects temporary rises in food and energy costs. So Tokyo is trying to stick with plan A and keep its rates ultra-low. In the meantime, officials hope a $65 billion stimulus package announced Friday will paradoxically cool the consumer price index by over a full percentage point via subsidies for electricity bills and petrol; there is talk of slashing consumption taxes.\par \par \uc2 But given 10-year U.S. bonds yield 4% while their Japanese equivalents pay next to nothing, investors have unsurprisingly moved money into dollar assets and out of yen, prompting Kuroda to reluctantly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The bigger problem, however, is in the bond market.\par \par \uc2 While Kuroda has enough firepower to keep the 10-year Japanese government bond yields below the self-imposed 0.25% cap, the market has pushed up yields along the rest of the curve; the 8-year now yields more. Traders are repeatedly testing the cap on the 10-year, and short positions against the yen are at their highest since May.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could tweak its policy by targeting a shorter tenor, for example. But with the entire curve creeping upwards, it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to believe this would make much difference. And as Kuroda tries to force the market to trade sovereign bonds at rates investors don\u8217\'20\'19t want, liquidity is drying up. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  already owns roughly half of the total sovereign market; its holdings of long-term government bonds have risen to 560 trillion yen ($3.8 trillion) in 2022 from 40 trillion yen in 2008.\par \par \uc2 A deep, liquid sovereign pool is critical to the functioning of any international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the yen, thanks to its low borrowing rates and easy convertibility, is one of the most popular. Passivity could do permanent damage to the yen\u8217\'20\'19s global stature.\par \par \uc2 Follow @petesweeneypro on \uc2 Twitter\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 Japan on Oct. 28 unveiled a stimulus package with spending worth 39 trillion yen ($265 billion) that it said would boost gross domestic product by around 4.6%, mostly in fiscal year 2023. An official said the measures also would suppress consumer price inflation by around 1.2 percentage points. Japan\u8217\'20\'19s annual consumer inflation rate was 3% in September.\par \par \uc2 The country\u8217\'20\'19s import bill grew more than 40% for a fifth straight month to hit the largest value on record, as a slumping exchange rate aggravated fuel import costs. Factory output fell for the first time in four months as manufacturers took a hit from rising costs for raw materials and the global economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Calculations by Reuters and U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission data show net short positioning on the yen has ballooned to 102,618 contracts, the highest level since May. The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has begun to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as the yen slides toward 150 per dollar, a plunge caused by widening interest rate spreads between the United States and Japan. The yen is currently at its weakest level since 1990.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Una Galani and Thomas Shum)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-3T13:03:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221031eiav02q85\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
