\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Japan likely spent record amount in October to prop up yen\b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2022\par 06:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yen weakened to 32-year lows at time of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  used 'stealth' approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Brokers estimate $24.9 bln spent in October, nearly double Sept\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Data eyed for clues on Japan's willingness to spend on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 31 (Reuters) - Japan will announce on Monday that it spent what was likely a record amount on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month to prop up the yen, with market players keen for clues about how much further the authorities might step in to ease the yen's sharp fall.\par \par \uc2 A compilation of estimates by Tokyo money market brokers indicates that Japan likely spent a record 5.4 trillion yen ($24.9 billion) over two consecutive trading days of unannounced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Oct. 21 and 24, in reaction to a steep drop in the yen to a 32-year low of 151.94 to the dollar on Oct. 21.\par \par \uc2 That would be nearly double the 2.8 trillion yen Tokyo spent last month in its first yen-buying and dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 The latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to trigger an immediate drop in the dollar of more than 7 yen, but the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since come under renewed pressure.\par \par \uc2 With solid U.S. consumer spending data focusing attention on persistent inflation and dampening expectations of slower interest rate hikes by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , while the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  remains committed to ultra-low interest rates, the dollar was rising again on Monday, up 0.44% at 148.08 yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, comprising monthly totals released around the end of each month and daily spending released in quarterly reports, is watched closely for clues on how much more Japan might be willing to spend in its forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce on Monday at 7 p.m. (1000 GMT) the total amount spent for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Sept. 29 to Oct. 27.\par \par \uc2 Monday's figures will draw additional scrutiny after the finance ministry refrained from commenting on its apparent actions in the market this month, taking a stealth approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It confirmed last month's yen-buying action immediately after it occurred.\par \par \uc2 But while the markets are keen to examine how much \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to commit to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there is little doubt that - at least for the foreseeable future - it has sufficient resources to continue stepping into the market.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, Masato Kanda, has said there was no limit to the authorities' resources for conducting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  held roughly $1.2 trillion in foreign reserves at the end of September, the second biggest after China, about one-tenth of which are held as deposits parked with foreign central banks and the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  and can be readily tapped for dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Moreoever, four-fifths of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's total foreign reserves are held as U.S. Treasuries, bought during bouts of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at those times when the yen was surging. Those can easily be converted into cash.\par \par \uc2 Other holdings include gold, reserves at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) and \uc2 IMF\uc2  special drawing rights (SDRs), although procuring dollar funds from these assets would take time, ministry officials say. (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Edmund Klamann) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-31T06:26:13.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221031eiav00re5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
