\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - October 28\b0\par\par\uc2 2,024 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2022\par 21:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 USD shrugs off data misses, EUR leads lower on less-hawkish \uc2 ECB\uc2  75bp hike\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par Market Briefs\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2  says \uc2 ECB\uc2  today decided to raise three interest rates by 75 basis points, says inflation remains far too high and will stay above target for an extended period \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2  says \uc2 ECB\uc2  will continue applying flexibility in reinvesting redemptions coming due in PEPP portfolio, with a view to countering risks to monetary policy transmission mechanism related to pandemic \u8226\'20\'22 Lagarde says might have to raise rates at several meetings, have not finished normalisation, may need to go beyond normalisation \u8226\'20\'22 Lagarde says risks to the inflation outlook are primarily on the upside, risks to growth are clearly on the downside, in particular in near term \u8226\'20\'22 ECB Doves says guidance tweak paves the ground for ending hikes in Dec in case of improved inflation outlook or possibly in March: sources \u8226\'20\'22 Italy Economy Minister: We trust \uc2 ECB\uc2  will take account of slowdown of EU economy in future monetary policy decisions \u8226\'20\'22 Fed seen slowing pace of rate hikes in December \u8226\'20\'22 Biden: We need to do more to bring prices down, Q3 GDP report is further evidence that our economic recovery is continuing to power forward \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. economy rebounds in Q3 on boost from trade, but demand stalling \u8226\'20\'22 US Q3 GDP Advance 2.6%, 2.4% f\u8216\'20\'18cast, -0.6% prev \u8226\'20\'22 US Q3 GDP Cons Spending Advance 1.4%, 2.0% prev \u8226\'20\'22 US Sep Durable Goods 0.4%, 0.6% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, -0.2% prev, 0.2% r\u8217\'20\'19vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Q3 Core PCE Prices Advance 4.5%, 4.5% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 4.7% prev; PCE Prices Advance 4.2%, 7.3% prev \u8226\'20\'22 US 22 Oct, w/e Initial Jobless Claims 217k, 220k f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 214k prev; Claims 4Wk Avg 219.00k, 212.25k prev \u8226\'20\'22 US 15 Oct, w/e Cont Jobless Claims 1.438 mln, 1.388 mln f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 1.385 mln prev, 1.383 mln r\u8217\'20\'19vsd \u8226\'20\'22 Canada's fiscal update won't make \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's job harder -sources \u8226\'20\'22 Global \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading hits record $7.5 trln a day - BIS survey Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 27 Oct 23:30 JP Oct CPI Tokyo Ex fresh food YY 3.1% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 2.8% prev \u8226\'20\'22 27 Oct 23:30 JP Oct CPI, Overall Tokyo 2.8% prev \u8226\'20\'22 27 Oct 23:30 JP Oct CPI Tokyo Excl Food & Energy Y/Y 0.8% prev; M/M 0.0% prev \u8226\'20\'22 27 Oct 23:30 JP Sep Jobs/Applicants Ratio 1.33 f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 1.32 prev \u8226\'20\'22 27 Oct 23:30 JP Sep Unemployment Rate 2.5% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 2.5% prev \u8226\'20\'22 28 Oct N/A JP 28 Oct JP BOJ Rate Decision -0.10% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, -0.10% prev \u8226\'20\'22 28 Oct 00:30 AU Q3 PPI QQ 1.4% prev; PPI YY 5.6% prev Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 N/A \uc2 BOJ\uc2  holds Monetary Policy Meeting in Tokyo \u8226\'20\'22 N/A \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas\uc2  issues Trimmed Mean PCE Price Index for September in Dallas \u8226\'20\'22 11:30 BoE\u8217\'20\'19s Afua Kyei speaks at Mansion House London Society of Chartered Accountants Pan Accountancy Lunch 2022 in London Macro Themes In Play \u8226\'20\'22 The dollar rose on Thursday as EUR/USD unwound 1% of its recent gains in the wake of a largely as expected 75bp \uc2 ECB\uc2  rate hike while the bank's view that "substantial progress" had been made in removing policy accommodation gave it a dovish tone. \u8226\'20\'22 After smaller-than-expected rate hikes by the \uc2 RBA\uc2  and \uc2 BOC\uc2 , markets are looking for evidence the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , Fed and \uc2 BoE\uc2  may be closer to slowing rate hikes. The dollar had been suffering amid reduced Fed hike pricing and the euro got its turn on Thursday. \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD's drop came as longs took profits after Wednesday's breakout above the 2022 downtrend line hit resistance by 1.0100. \u8226\'20\'22 Friday's U.S. employment cost index and core PCE reports, which inform Fed policy making, look pivotal ahead of month-end and Wednesday's Fed meeting. \u8226\'20\'22 A 75bp hike remains expected, though terminal rate pricing is shy of 4.8% from above 5% on Oct. 20 as smaller rate increases are expected from December onward. \u8226\'20\'22 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is priced to hike 50bp in December, with a total of roughly 50bp after that for a 2.5% policy peak. \u8226\'20\'22 Treasury yields, dragged down by bunds after the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , received no support from U.S. GDP, durable goods, and jobless claims. \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling fell 0.5% in choppy trading, retreating from this week's hefty gains as UK fiscal and political risk premia evaporated. \u8226\'20\'22 The \uc2 BoE\uc2  meets next week with a 75bp hike mostly favored versus the more than 100bp increase seen amid the recent mini-budget ructions. Total hikes of roughly 250bp are priced before a Q3 peak. \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY fell 0.2%, tugged lower by falling Treasury-JGB yields because JGB yields move little as other major bond markets shift. The BoJ will not change its ultra-easy policy on Friday, even as the Japanese government readies roughly $200bln in spending to offset inflation and boost growth. Commentary and Analysis EUR/USD-Bulls run out of gas with dollar, \uc2 ECB\uc2  help \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD hit 1.00935 on EBS overnight, NY opened near 1.0050, slide extended \u8226\'20\'22 Overnight drop aided by US rate, US$ rise; \uc2 ECB\uc2  aided the slide in NY \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2  hiked 75bps as expected; likely drove buy the rumor sell the fact trade \u8226\'20\'22 EZ rates slid as some investors saw as \uc2 ECB\uc2  not as hawkish \u8226\'20\'22 Pair traded to 0.9970 on EBS and held nearby late in the session \u8226\'20\'22 Further drop likely avoided as US rates, US$ softened on data \u8226\'20\'22 Techs mixed; daily RSI falls & monthly rises, pair above 10-, 21-, 55-DMAs \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD's post-\uc2 ECB\uc2  fall may be a buying opportunity USD/JPY-Consolidates yield spread driven loss after \uc2 ECB\uc2 , pre-\uc2 ECI\uc2 , PCE and Fed \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY fell 0.2%, dragged down by Tsy-JGB yields spread slide post-\uc2 ECB\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Huge drops in bund yields spilled over into lower Tsy yields \u8226\'20\'22 Because JGB yield barely move due to BoJ policies, Tsy-JGB spreads fell \u8226\'20\'22 There's no expectation for the BoJ to reduce easing at Friday's meeting \u8226\'20\'22 The dollar was up vs the euro and most other majors where yields do adjust \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY's 145.11 EBS low Thur found buyers above Oct. 21's 144.50 spike low \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MoF no longer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and market being driven by yields spreads \u8226\'20\'22 2022's uptrend under some pressure after a sub-21-DMA close Wednesday \u8226\'20\'22 A close below the kijun at 146.125 could see cloud top by 142 in play \u8226\'20\'22 But likely need soft \uc2 ECI\uc2  and PCE data Friday to undermine the carry trade \u8226\'20\'22 2-yr Tsy-JGB yields at 4.37% vs 4.63% peak; Oct's 4.1% low is key support Sterling dips as bulls lighten longs ahead of key Fed, BoE c.bank meetings \u8226\'20\'22 GBP$ soft into NY close, -0.3% at 1.1595; NorAm range 1.1634-1.1550 \u8226\'20\'22 Pair giving back gains after hitting 6-wk high despite less-hawkish Fed view \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling bulls temper enthusiasm in mid-1.16s ahead of c.banks \u8226\'20\'22 Pair offered ahead of upper 30-d Bolli (1.1670), daily cloud top 1.1752 \u8226\'20\'22 Support at daily cloud base 1.1561, Thursday low 1.1550, 55-DMA 1.1447 \u8226\'20\'22 Bears in control while above 1.1285 the 50% Fib of 1.0925-1.1645 rise \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/GBP -0.63% to 0.8614, Thurs range 0.8686-15; mkt wanted more hawkish 75bp hike from \uc2 ECB\uc2  AUD/USD-Bulls pause but will likely run again \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD fell in Europe, NY opened near 0.6455, slide extended to 0.64275 \u8226\'20\'22 Rally ensued after US data; US rates, US$ softened \u8226\'20\'22 Equities, commodities lifted; USD/CNH fell below 7.2250 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD rallied toward 0.6505 then neared 0.6485 late in the session \u8226\'20\'22 Daily doji formed, daily RSI diverged, suggests a drop is due \u8226\'20\'22 Rising monthly RSI, monthly long legged doji implies rally to resume \u8226\'20\'22 Monthly techs likely to trump daily signals which means dip buying likely EUR/USD's post-\uc2 ECB\uc2  fall may be a buying opportunity EUR/USD fell on Thursday, sliding below the daily cloud base after the \uc2 ECB\uc2  hiked 75bp as expected but the dip may be a buying opportunity due to U.S. rate influences and Italian-German yield spread tightening. After recent gains, EUR/USD's fall resembled a sell-the-fact reaction to a rate increase that merely met the consensus forecast. \uc2 ECB\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  said that additional rate hikes are coming but also noted risks to growth are on the downside. EUR/USD downside momentum was not maintained however as U.S. interest rates softened on data implying the economy is weakening. Softer rates suggest investors are anticipating a less hawkish stance from the Fed in the future. German and Italian bond yields fell but Italian yields outpaced German yields, which tightened the 10-year spread. Tighter spreads may be sign concerns about Italian financing conditions may be receding, which should remove a downside risk for EUR/USD. EUR/USD's up trend off the Sept. 26 low is intact and monthly technicals are turning bullish, keeping alive the possibility of a rally towards the 1.0500 area. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3Nfx9VQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3U2JLBO"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Dollar cloud support could vanish if \uc2 ECI\uc2 , PCE come out soft The dollar index rose on Thursday, finding support from the daily ichimoku cloud and falling bund-Treasury yield spreads in the wake of the \uc2 ECB\uc2  meeting and U.S. data, but if Friday's U.S. ECI and PCE reports favor the Fed ending rate hikes sooner, the floor underneath the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will become shaky. The dollar index rebounded from a 109.53 low by the daily cloud top at 109.45, reprising the supporting role the cloud's played this year. The index's majority component, EUR/USD fell after 1.00885/935 highs on EBS Wednesday, and Thursday struggled with the cloud top, last at 1.0088, as well as the upper 10-day Bolli at 1.0010. Sterling's rally hit 1.1645 on Wednesday in the middle of its daily cloud. Aside from the cloud, which falls away rapidly after Thursday, sterling needs to close above its downtrend line since February that Wednesday's 1.16385 high hit. Prices haven't been above daily cloud since Russia invaded Ukraine in February. U.S. data allowed Fed rate hike pricing to recede further. If Friday's PCE and \uc2 ECI\uc2  inflation reports extend that trend, dollar cloud support could vanish, with weekly props by 108 eyed. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3TK99fI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3fahtq2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Chart of the Day (GBP/USD) Sterling fell on Thursday from an overnight highs of 1.1645 but remained anchored by 1.16 as traders consider a lower Fed rate hike path and book profits on GBP/USD's run-up from last Friday's low of 1.1060 ahead of next week's Fed and \uc2 BoE\uc2  meetings. Sterling's 12.74% rise from post-mini-budget lows at 1.0327 to Thursday's high at 1.1645, including a six-big-figure rise since PM Rishi Sunak declared his intention to seek the premiership, is ripe for profit-taking as traders lighten positions ahead of the key \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings, which may indicate a less-hawkish rate path in 2023. Fundamentals aside, the pound is running into technical resistance at the upper 30-day Bolli by 1.1674 and the daily cloud top at 1.1753. GBP/USD has held below both indicators since the \uc2 BoE\uc2  dialed back its inflation-fighting vigor in March 2023. Despite the recent sterling rally from all-time lows, GBP IMM futures indicate traders remain significantly short and GBP/USD may continue to rise toward early 2022 highs above 1.20 as shorts unwind amid increased UK political and economic stability, and the end of the Fed's hiking cycle in 2023. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3zmaX6C"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-30T19:00:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221027eiar04fvl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
