\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The 'British disease' is spreading\b0\par\par\uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Public debt soars out of control. Central banks are trying to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And markets are losing confidence in political leaders addicted to printed money. As a description of the UK in recent weeks, it's painfully familiar. And yet, here's the twist. In recent weeks, it has begun to apply to Japan, to China, and very soon it could apply to France and Italy as well.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the "British disease," as we might call it, is already spreading around the world. A whole host of major economies face the same toxic mix of mounting debt, skyrocketing interest bills, aging workforces and bloated, unaffordable welfare systems that can only be kept afloat by central banks that keep the printing presses running until the financial markets agree with them. The UK may have been the first to collapse, but it will not be the last.\par \par \uc2 The UK may have been focused on the endless internal Conservative Party psycho-drama over the last week, and wondering how long the next Prime Minister could survive in office, but the markets had more or less already moved on. When \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  left the stage, the pound barely moved. In fact, the action had already moved elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 This week, the Japanese yen fell below the psychologically crucial 150 to the dollar mark, its lowest level since 1990, when the country's economic miracle was beginning to crumble. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market last month with its first direct action to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a quarter of a century. This week, it declined to say whether it would support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, but it was also buying government bonds to keep yields from rising. If anyone thought UK debts were bad, they haven't looked at Japan, where the debt-to-GDP ratio has reached an alarming 259% and where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already bought assets - a proxy for printed money - amounting to 100% of GDP. Also in the Pacific, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has told major financial institutions to prepare to sell dollars against the renminbi to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from depreciating further, while flooding the economy with money to stem a real estate slump. And \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  reactivated a $1 billion "bond stabilization fund," an inauspicious sign, while \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already begun to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets.\par \par \uc2 Here in Europe, the picture is even worse. True, most EU leaders have laughed off the UK problem, and we certainly deserve it. Still, bond yields continue to rise steadily across the continent. Greece, with a debt-to-GDP ratio of 190%, saw its 10-year yield rise to 5.2% on Friday. Italy, with a 160% ratio, saw its yield rise to 4.8%. Unlike the eurozone crisis of a decade ago, the core is just as bad. The yield on French debt, the third highest in the world after U.S. and Japanese debt, rose above 3 percentage points on Friday, up from 0.2 percentage points at the start of the year, and the highest level in more than a decade (it must be the most tempting short trade in the world right now). Even the mighty German economy is starting to falter. The yield on German 10-year debt rose above 2.5 percentage points on Friday for the first time since 2011. All the money the French and German governments are happily borrowing to bail out their energy markets looks like it will suddenly be much harder to pay back.\par \par \uc2 Local circumstances are always different. The UK is struggling to adjust to its exit from the EU while its ruling party is embroiled in a civil war. Japan is still cleaning up the mess from the crash of three decades ago at the same time it became the first advanced country with a shrinking population. China is facing the consequences of limiting population growth before it is fully developed while substituting exports for domestic demand. And the eurozone is stuck with the world's most dysfunctional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , wild divergences in inflation rates among its members, and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is so far behind in controlling inflation that rates have only risen 1 point. In all cases, however, the basic problem is the same. Central banks have kept economies afloat for over a decade with printed money, allowing governments to become ever more extravagant, pumping up asset bubbles and letting productivity and competitiveness steadily decline. In the wake of the Covid closures, that has now translated into alarming rates of inflation, and by slowing down everything we thought could go on forever has suddenly become unaffordable.\par \par \uc2 The "British disease" will not, in reality, have an easy cure. Interest rates will have to return to normal levels, even if that means that house prices fall and many businesses go bust. Governments will have to raise the retirement age and cut welfare budgets so that spending is brought back under control and people are encouraged to work longer. Businesses will have to figure out how to dramatically improve productivity so that they can continue to expand even as their costs rise. Consumers will have to get used to permanently higher prices, and to paying for everything they need on real wages that have fallen sharply. It will be hard work, and it won't be much fun. But there will be no choice. Actually, the UK may have gone down first, as investors looked at the national balance sheet and decided to get as far away from it as possible. But the "British disease" is global, and many other countries will be caught soon.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221026eiaq008de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
