\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 No time to decouple from the Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Last week was another turbulent week for international financial markets. We have had a lot of them this year, unfortunately. While the strength of corporate reports 'saved' the major stock markets from declines, global bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets saw very high volatility. For example, yields on 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds rose more than 20 basis points (0.20 percentage points) during the same period, which is a considerable movement for this type of instrument, reaching 4.33 percent, a level not seen since 2008. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the other hand, showed very high levels of volatility. The Japanese yen, for example, surpassed 150 yen to the dollar, a level not seen since the early nineties. Even the main financial news media are saying that it is very likely that the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling dollars from its international reserves last Friday, in order to mitigate the yen's weakness for the first time since 1997-1998.\par \par \uc2  Recall that just a few weeks ago, volatility in the \uc2 British government\uc2  bond market (Gilts) prompted significant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to stabilize it.\par \par \uc2 In this very tense environment, the Mexican peso continued to show gallantry and not only did it not depreciate last week, but ended the week with an appreciation of 0.54 percent. In my opinion, there are several fundamental reasons why the peso has shown such resilience. However, one of them, and perhaps the most important, has been the actions of the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 , as well as what it is expected to do going forward. In fact, at the beginning of the month I wrote two columns on this subject ("\uc2 Banxico\uc2  does the right thing", Part 1 and Part 2, October 2022). There is nervousness among international financial market participants. A global recession scenario for 2023 is no longer seen as a risk; today it is the baseline scenario. It is perceived that the actions being taken by central banks to combat high inflation levels, especially in developed countries, are aimed at cooling the global economy. The risk is that in addition to the recession predicted by the consensus of analysts, the monetary tightening could go beyond what is necessary and engender a global financial stress event. In these times of global financial nervousness, spiced by a plethora of geopolitical risks, investors tend to seek refuge in safe-haven assets, such as the dollar and short-term dollar-denominated debt instruments.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso has behaved 'like the big boys', but this can change if market participants perceive a fiscal deterioration -as in the UK a few weeks ago-, a deterioration in external accounts -as in the Andean countries last July-, a political deterioration -as in Colombia in recent weeks-, or when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts differently or even contrary to what the US Federal Reserve Bank (Fed) and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ) are doing, as has been the case with the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 . The latter is perceived as a monetary deterioration and I would like to emphasize that this did not occur in an emerging country, but in Japan. In short, 'el horno no est\u225\'00\'E1 para bollos' in terms of \uc2 Banxico\uc2  being able to carry out or even signal a decoupling from the Fed ("\uc2 Banxico\uc2 : It will be very difficult to decouple from the Fed", July 12), especially since inflation in Mexico is not only almost three times the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 's inflation target and although it is declining, it has not yet shown a clear downward trajectory.\par \par \uc2 Last week on Banorte's 'Norte Econ\u243\'00\'F3mico' podcast, its host, Alejandro Padilla, interviewed \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  Deputy Governor \uc2 Gerardo Esquivel\uc2 . I understand the Deputy Governor's concern about the weakness of the Mexican economy. Likewise, his argument that \uc2 Banxico\uc2  started its rate hike cycle much earlier than the Fed (June 2021 vs. March 2022) and that it is feasible that inflation will fall in the future, both in Mexico and in the United States, seems very reasonable to me. Moreover, in the past I have agreed on many occasions with Deputy Governor Esquivel's positions, such as his call not to raise the benchmark rate above what the Fed was raising the Fed Funds rate a few months ago. Notwithstanding the above, on this occasion I differ. I do not believe that \uc2 Banxico\uc2  should decouple from the Fed. I believe that the difference of opinion has to do with the monetary policy transmission channel, as well as the reasons why investment is at very low levels in Mexico. In my opinion, the exchange rate and expectations channels are the ones that really work in our country. I believe that the aggregate demand and credit channels are almost irrelevant for inflation control. Also, I do not believe that investment in our country is at historically low levels due to monetary policy. In my opinion, investment has not recovered to 2018 levels mainly due to the rhetoric of the current administration. Thus, I do not believe that investment would show a stronger recovery, if \uc2 Banxico\uc2  were to reduce the benchmark rate from 9.25 (current) to 3.00 percent, for example.\par \par \uc2 * The author is Chief Economist for Latin America at \uc2 Barclays Bank\uc2  and a member of the Mexican Economic Cycle Dating Committee.\par \par \uc2 * The opinions expressed in this column are personal.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221026eiaq007lt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
