\par\par \b \uc2 Heard on the Street: Japan Is Uniquely Positioned to Withstand  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Weakness -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacky Wong \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2022\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B15\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen is bumping up against its weakest level in more than three decades. That weakness will persist for some time -- and could deepen. Officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely stand pat when they meet later this week, keeping ultralow interest-rate policies in place despite market tremors.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen is hovering around 150 to the dollar, after briefly dipping below that milestone last week. That's the weakest since 1990, when Japan's historic economic boom started to come to an end. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to buy the yen in recent days, but has been quite coy about it.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo hasn't made any official announcement since disclosing that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen last month, but recent sudden sharp intraday moves suggest that the government may have dipped into the markets again. Even though 150 seems like an important psychological level, the government likely hasn't got a "line in the sand" that it is willing to defend at all costs. Nonetheless, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- especially when stealthily done -- can create uncertainty for market participants that may slow the pace of depreciation.\par \par \uc2 The only way the yen will really reverse its downward trend is when the monetary policies of the U.S. and Japan start to converge again. Right now, they remain miles apart. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is raising interest rates aggressively, while the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has kept interest rates near zero.\par \par \uc2 That divergence is causing some real pain for the Japanese economy. The cheap yen has made imports more expensive: Japan's imports jumped 46% year-over-year in September as prices for crude oil and natural gas surged. Japan, a major exporter, has had a trade deficit for 14 consecutive months -- and it has widened this year.\par \par \uc2 But the reason the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  can and likely will continue to swim against the tide is that the nation's consumer inflation rate was still a relatively mild 3% in September, unlike most other developed economies. Moreover, most of that was driven by imported fresh food and energy. Excluding those, inflation was at 1.8%. And the pace of increase has been slowing for both categories in recent months, especially fresh food.\par \par \uc2 Wage growth, in particular, remains anemic: Average monthly cash earnings grew 1.7% in August. The Bank of Japan won't start tightening until there are signs that broad-based domestic inflation is starting to take hold.\par \par \uc2 So far, that still seems far off. That will keep the yen weak -- and possibly testing new lows -- in the near future.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacky Wong at jacky.wong@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (October 26, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 26, 2022 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221026eiaq000n1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
