\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares rise on hopes of rate hike slowdown\b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2022\par 04:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 26 (Reuters) - Asian shares edged higher on Wednesday as investors clung to hopes that the pace of U.S. and global rate hikes will start to slow, though U.S. futures dropped after disappointing results from tech giants \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 E-mini futures for the S&P 500 fell 1% in early trade after \uc2 Google\uc2 -owner \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  posted softer-than-expected ad sales after the bell and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  missed expected revenue forecasts, possible early signs of a slowdown in the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was up 1%, led by a bounce in Hong Kong, while Japan's Nikkei rose 1.1% by mid-morning.\par \par \uc2 The mainland Chinese benchmark index advanced 1%, while Hong Kong stocks rose 2%, attempting another rebound after Monday's deep sell-off in Chinese assets by global investors worried about Beijing's policy direction.\par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's new leadership team has raised worries that a more powerful Party leadership will increasingly prioritise the state at the cost of the private sector, and keep tough zero-COVID policies in place well into next year.\par \par \uc2 U.S. economic data on Tuesday showed slowing home price growth and souring consumer confidence, with some signs that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's aggressive interest rate hikes are starting to cool the labour market.\par \par \uc2 "Continuing the theme of bad (economic) news is good news (for risk markets), U.S. equities are continuing to bask in the afterglow of last Friday\u8217\'20\'19s hints of a step-down in the pace of Fed tightening," \uc2 Ray Attrill\uc2 , head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  in Sydney, said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Traders and economists predict another 75 basis point (bps) increase from the Fed next Wednesday, but the view is growing for a slowing to half a point in December.\par \par \uc2 Treasuries rallied sharply overnight, with the yield on benchmark 10-year \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt down more than 12 bps. It was steady at 4.0937% on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, inflation raced to a 32-year high last quarter as the cost of home building and gas surged. The surprise added pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse a recent dovish turn, though markets doubt there will be a dramatic shift.\par \par \uc2 Support for the Aussie dollar was small and fleeting, leaving it more or less steady at $0.6386. Three-year Australian government bond futures were knocked from peaks but managed to hold steady for the day at 96.400.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the dollar flirted with a three-week low versus major peers, while sterling hung close to the six-week peak reached on Tuesday after new British Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  pledged to lead the country out of an economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 The pound was last trading at $1.1445, down 0.19% on the day, but not far from Tuesday's high of $1.1500, a level last seen on Sept. 15.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen weakened 0.30% versus the greenback at 148.39 per dollar. The beaten-down \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had touched a 32-year low at 151.94 on Friday, but retreated after two bouts of suspected \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  either side of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bonds rallied sharply after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  again said it would increase bond buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oil prices fell as industry data showed U.S. crude oil stockpiles rose more than expected, reinforcing fears of a global recession that would cut demand.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures for December dropped $1.17, or 1.3%, to $92.35 a barrel at 0111 GMT, after climbing 26 cents in the previous session. U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude futures for December delivery were down 88 cents, or 1%, to $84.44 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee in Singapore; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-26T03:49:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221026eiaq00lj3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
