\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sags as bets build for less hawkish Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2022\par 03:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 26 (Reuters) - The dollar wallowed near a three-week low versus major peers on Wednesday as more signs of economic weakness in the United States fanned speculation about a less hawkish \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sterling hung close to the six-week peak reached on Tuesday after new British Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  pledged to lead the country out of an economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 The euro also remained near a six-week high, trading less than half a cent from parity with the greenback. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided policy on Thursday and is widely expected to raise rates by 75 basis points.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index - which measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against six peers, including sterling, the euro and the yen - was little changed at 111.01, near the previous session's trough of 110.75, the lowest level since Oct. 5.\par \par \uc2 Data overnight showed that U.S. home prices sank in August as surging mortgage rates sapped demand, amid recent signs that Fed rate increases are already working to slow the world's biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 Traders and economists predict another 75 basis point increase next Wednesday, but the view is growing for a slowing to half a point in December.\par \par \uc2 "I'm still in two minds as to whether we can say we've seen a peak in the U.S. dollar," but "evidence of a slowdown is building," said \uc2 Ray Attrill\uc2 , head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the market gets really comfortable with a Fed pivot - if that's what stepping down to 50 basis points is, and potentially ending a tightening cycle south of 5% early next year - then it will be time to call time on U.S. dollar strength, but I'd like to get through the Fed messaging next week before coming to that conclusion."\par \par \uc2 U.S. long-term Treasury yields continued their descent from last week's multi-year highs at 4.338%, sliding to 4.0833% in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 That put particular pressure on the dollar versus the yen , due to its sensitivity to U.S. rates, with the pair steady at 147.99 following a 0.7% slide from Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar reached a 32-year top at 151.94 yen on Friday, but was then beaten back as far as 144.55 amid two bouts of suspected \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  either side of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Even so, fundamentals still favor a weaker yen with the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  expected on Friday to keep stimulus settings unchanged, running counter to monetary tightening by developed-market peers.\par \par \uc2 Sterling eased 0.21% to $1.1448, but was still close to Tuesday's high of $1.1500, a level last seen on Sept. 15.\par \par \uc2 The euro slipped 0.14% to $0.99545, after jumping to its highest since Oct. 5 on Tuesday at $0.9995.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was 0.13% lower at $0.6386, despite getting a short-lived pop to as high as $0.6412 after quarterly consumer inflation data narrowly topped economists' estimates, putting some pressure on the Reserve Bank ahead of a policy meeting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie reached the highest since Oct. 7 at $0.6412 in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 "The Aussie's weak attempt to rally on above-expectations core CPI is not a promising sign for its near term prospects," said Sean Callow, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "A further pullback in the U.S. dollar seems to be the Aussie's best chance of sustaining pushes above $0.64. Otherwise, it's back to trading either side of $0.63."\par \par \uc2 Cryptocurrencies were also firm after sharp rallies on Tuesday amid dollar weakness. Bitcoin was 0.14% higher at $20,116 after a 3.9% jump overnight. Ether was up 0.45% at $1,466.30, building on Tuesday's 8.7% surge.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Buckland. Editing by Gerry Doyle) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-26T02:53:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221026eiaq00dyt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
