\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Europe  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - October 25\b0\par\par\uc2 1,771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2022\par 06:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. dollar eases with risk resilient in Asia\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par Market Briefs\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 POLL - Fed to hike by 75 bps again on Nov. 2, should pause when inflation halves \u8226\'20\'22 POLL - Rising U.S. rates pose biggest risk for Japan but recession unlikely \u8226\'20\'22 PREVIEW-Unfazed by yen's slump, \uc2 BOJ\uc2  seen keeping ultra-low rates \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  FinMin Suzuki - BOJ easing and govt \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not contradictory \u8226\'20\'22 BOJ Kuroda \u8211\'20\'13 Recent, one-sided yen weakening is not desirable for economy \u8211\'20\'13 govt off\u8217\'20\'19l \u8226\'20\'22 Japan keeps warning on market volatility, raises view on capex in report \u8226\'20\'22 China's yuan weakens to near 15-year low in aftermath of party congress \u8226\'20\'22 China makes it easier for firms to borrow from overseas as yuan drops \u8226\'20\'22 Recovery of UK gilts shows credibility coming back: \uc2 BoE\uc2 's Ramsden \u8226\'20\'22 Britain's new PM Sunak will get little leeway from markets, investors say \u8226\'20\'22 Australia's budget to downgrade growth, keep spending in check Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 08:00 DE Oct Ifo Biz Climate 83.3 f'cast, 84.3 prev; Curr Cond 92.4 f'cast, 94.5 prev \u8226\'20\'22 08:00 DE Oct Ifo Expectations 75.0 f'cast, 75.2 prev \u8226\'20\'22 10:00 GB Oct CBI Trends - Orders -12 f'cast, -2 prev; Q4 CBI Business Optimism -21 prev Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 08:55 BoE Pill speaks at London ONS conference \u8226\'20\'22 11:00 ECB Centeno speaks at Lisbon conference Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY again in tight range, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch still on \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY in relatively tight 148.50-149.10 EBS range in Asia \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch still on, stealth now maybe the norm \u8226\'20\'22 Caution likely to prevail, 147/148-150 range maybe for now \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese exporter Gotobi Tokyo fix supply, offers trail up above 149.00 \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese importer, some investor, spec bids on dips sub-148.50 \u8226\'20\'22 Nearby option expiries today chock-a-block, to help contain action? \u8226\'20\'22 Massive at 145.00-10, 50-75, 147.00, 40-50, 148.00, 149.40-50, 150 \u8226\'20\'22 US yields up from lows yesterday, Treasury 10s @4.212%, 2s @4.499% \u8226\'20\'22 Asia mostly risk-on, Nikkei +0.8% @27,201, E-Minis -0.1% @3804 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY 146.84-147.17 EBS, GBP/JPY 167.85-168.69, AUD/JPY 93.89-94.43 EUR/USD-Maintains firm tone through Asian session \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened +0.13% at 0.9875 after getting support from cross flows \u8226\'20\'22 It moved up to 0.9898 in Asia before running into offers at 0.9900 \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon the EUR/USD is settled around 0.9880/85 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD showing resilience despite bleak economic outlook for EZ \u8226\'20\'22 Key resistance at descending trend-line that comes in at 0.9942 \u8226\'20\'22 A clear break above 0.9945 would suggest a bottom is in place \u8226\'20\'22 Support comes in at the 10-day MA at 0.9808 and 21-day MA at 0.9793 \u8226\'20\'22 A close below 0.9790 would renew pressure to the downside AUD/USD-Claws back some ground before easing from session high \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened -1.28% at 0.6313 after AUD fell against every major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Renewed China growth concerns weighed heavily on the AUD \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD rallied to 0.6341 in Asia before a risk off tone capped \u8226\'20\'22 E-minis eased 0.15% while Dalian iron ore fell 2.40% \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD settled around 0.6320/25 into the afternoon session \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD support at the 10-day MA at 0.6296 and close below would be bearish \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 21-day MA at 0.6366 with sellers above 0.6400 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD fate may be linked to China sentiment in the coming days GBP/USD-1.1300 a magnet, ahead of Sunak's policy details \u8226\'20\'22 +0.15% in a 1.1282-1.1325 range with consistent flow on D3 \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling consolidates, awaiting the new governments monetary policy \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BoE\uc2  will 'take the actions necessary' to fight inflation \u8226\'20\'22 Charts, daily momentum studies climb, 21 day Bollinger bands contract \u8226\'20\'22 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages edge higher - a modest positive setup \u8226\'20\'22 1.1440-1.1540 is major resistance, recent highs and 61.8% Aug-Sep fall \u8226\'20\'22 1.1187 21 day moving average and this week's 1.1060 low initial support \u8226\'20\'22 Economic policies the trigger for a sterling breakout BoJ stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  maybe new norm \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently switched tack in how it conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Rather than confirm action as in the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  have left the market guessing as to whether or not it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a particular occasion. Granted, reports of the size of transactions on Friday and Monday, and moves in USD/JPY, leave no doubt in the minds of most that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually took place. That said, this new strategy leaves open the possibility of smaller, perhaps more frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and could keep market participants cautious for longer. Banks, which operate on behalf of Japanese authorities during \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are likely to remain mum on whether they were used for fear of being blacklisted by the BoJ. Monday saw especially good USD sales by one major Japanese mega-bank. Friday may have seen all three of Japan's major banks in action given the reported scope of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Such a strategy by Japanese authorities is not surprising given the market propensity to view \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as opportunities to buy USD anew. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday took USD/JPY down from 151.95 to 146.23 and was followed by a bounce later to 148.46. Monday's Asia action, which took USD/JPY from 149.70 to 145.56, then saw a bounce later to 149.45 in London. No doubt a number of speculators were forced out of USD longs post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Japanese authorities are likely to continue with this strategy in an effort to punish speculators and slow JPY depreciation in light of diverging \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Economic policies the trigger for a sterling breakout Sterling has been in a 1.0925-1.1493 range since late September. The market response to \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 's economic policies will likely trigger a breakout. Sunak becomes Prime Minister amid an impending UK recession. The Ukraine war, EU/UK tensions surrounding Brexit, and the Northern Ireland protocol multiply Sunak's problems. Sunak has not released any firm policies, but is expected to announce spending cuts and tax increases. Various members of the Conservative party prioritize differently a broad range of economic and political ideologies, so whatever he announces, there will be dissent. Interest rate differentials are key for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and upcoming events suggest GBP/USD yield spreads should not change dramatically, unless the Federal Reserve or the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  surprise with a dovish outlook. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  will take the steps needed to get inflation back to target; BOEWATCH prices a 75 basis point hike to 3.00% on November 3rd at 69.53% and a 100 bps hike at 30.47%. This follows the Federal Reserve rate decision on November 2nd; a 75 bps hike is priced at 95.5% by CME FedWatch. Technically, daily momentum studies climb; the 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages edge north - a modest positive setup below major resistance. The 1.1440-1.1540 span contains recent highs and the 61.8% Fibonacci retracement of the August-September fall. The 1.0925 October low is pivotal support. AUD/USD dented by fresh China growth concerns The Australian dollar was the worst performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, as its role as a proxy for China sentiment drew waves of selling. The 20th China Communist Party Congress concluded Sunday with China President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  securing a precedent-breaking third leadership term. This was factored in, but global investors were rattled by the introduction of a leadership team stacked with loyalists - prioritising China's rivalry with the U.S. over economic reform. The market reaction was severe, with Hong Kong's Hang Seng index diving around 6.5% while U.S.-listed shares of Chinese companies collapsed over 10%. The AUD fell 1.28% against the USD and 1.41% against the Euro despite a strong gain on Wall Street on hopes of a dovish Fed pivot and weak EZ PMI data - suggesting the Euro zone is heading for a recession. Key for the direction of the Australian dollar will be whether investor concerns over China's growth trajectory translate into weaker commodity prices. A fall in iron ore, copper and energy prices would undermine Australia's very strong terms of trade. The AUD/USD managed to close above the 10-day moving average, which came in at 0.6291 on Monday and ascends to 0.6297 today. A close below that line would warn that momentum is shifting to the downside and sets up for another move towards the 2022 trend low at 0.6170. USD/CNH catapulted to new peak as \uc2 PBOC\uc2  loosens fix USD/CNH catapulted to a new record high of 7.3650 after the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  set Tuesday's daily yuan benchmark significantly weaker, implying a loosening of the tight leash it had on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the Communist Party Congress. Tuesday's USD/CNY fix was set at 7.1668, substantially higher than Reuters' estimate for 7.1348, which factored in aggressive damping as was apparently employed over the past two weeks. The shift follows the weekend conclusion of the congress, which extended President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's leadership tenure but triggered a strong adverse reaction from financial markets which fear economic and market reforms will now take a back seat to ideological policies. While Chinese stocks continue to melt, the onshore CNY has breached the 7.3000 psychological barrier to notch a 15-year low against the dollar. On a trade-weighted basis, the renminbi index is due for another leg lower as the yuan and Chinese equities get shunned by foreign investors, while other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hover or strengthen modestly with this week's bout of improved risk appetite for ex-China assets. Further yuan weakness is ahead, even if USD strength abates towards year-end as traders again look for toned-down hawkishess by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 . USD/CNY bulls might not find much opposition now that it appears Chinese authorities are returning some control over the yuan to market forces. There isn't any meaningful technical resistance till 7.4223, the 61.8% Fibonacci retracement of the 2005-2014 slide.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \