\par\par \b \uc2 Japan likely spent around $6 bln for Oct 24 suspected  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2022\par 11:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 25 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely spent up to 900 billion yen ($6 billion) on its second straight day of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, bringing the total for yen-buying action since last month to as much as 9.2 trillion yen, according to market estimates on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Authorities' so-called stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in rapid succession has underscored their resolve in fighting what they have characterised as "intolerable and speculative" yen-selling, although the impact has proved short-lived.\par \par \uc2 Japan has the world's second-largest foreign reserves after China, at $1.238 trillion. But only a fraction is readily available for use, in the form of funds such as deposits parked with central banks and the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Sept. 22, Japan spent up to a record 2.8 trillion yen in its first yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998, equivalent to about a tenth of the $136 billion in immediately available reserve funds as of September.\par \par \uc2 That followed an estimated 5.4 trillion to 5.5 trillion yen of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday, according to estimates by Tokyo money market brokerage firms derived from their daily calculation of the outstanding balance of excess reserves at the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  after accounting for transactions involving the private sector.\par \par \uc2 The reduction in reserves excluding these is estimated as the amount of yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which absorbs the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from excess reserves parked at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry confirmed last month's yen-buying action but has kept mum on whether they have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since. Sources said the one last Friday was responsible for the dollar's falling more than 7 yen after touching a 32-year high of 151.94 yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's drop quickly reversed, however, prompting renewed stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday as it again approached 150 yen. The greenback was trading near 149 yen on Tuesday after hitting 145.28 yen on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The ministry will announce next Monday the amount it spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between Sept. 29 and Oct. 27. Quarterly data in early November will show the amount of daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the July-September quarter. ($1 = 148.8700 yen) (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto and Daiki Iga Editing by Chang-Ran Kim) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-25T10:12:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221025eiap01qi1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
