higher \u8226\'20\'22 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages edge north - signals are positive \u8226\'20\'22 Today's 1.1402 high and Friday's 1.1060 low initial support, resistance Week Ahead-CB meetings, UK politics, major US earnings lead It will be a very busy week, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings all over the world \u8211\'20\'13 including Japan, Canada and Europe. Meanwhile the UK Tory party appears ready to identify a leader to replace \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  and Wall Street will react to the largest serving for US corporate earnings. The Bank of Japan is expected to remain on hold at extremely accommodating settings, as the MOF continues to rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the slide in the Yen. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is expected to hike 75 basis points for the second straight meeting, as EZ inflation continues to hover around 10% and showing little sign of peaking. The market will pay close attention to the statement and comments from \uc2 ECB\uc2  President Lagarde, as there is a fierce debate within the \uc2 ECB\uc2  and the size of future rate hikes. The markets were expecting a 50-basis point hike from the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  before last week\u8217\'20\'19s CPI. But the hotter than expected result has shifted the odds in favour of a 75 BP hike. Before the CPI release the market was pricing in a 30% chance of a 75 BP hike and it jumped to a 60% chance after the release. The ruling UK Conservative Party moved a step closer to replacing outgoing PM \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  after former PM Boris Johnson pulled out of the race. \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  looks assured of becoming the next PM and the GBP/USD reacted positively to the news when the market opened on Monday. It will be a huge week for US company earnings with the four largest S&P companies by market capitalisation will report. \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  will be reporting along with the likes of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Investors will likely focus on forward guidance as well as earnings. Key data in the week ahead: EZ flash PMIs, US GDP, Australia CPI On Monday the release of flash PMIs for the Euro Zone will likely show manufacturing and services falling deeper into contraction territory as the slowdown in Europe gathers pace. Other European data includes German IFO, German Q3 flash GDP and German CPI. Flash \uc2 PMI\uc2  is the only data of note out of the UK in the week ahead. In the U.S. it will be Q3 advance GDP that will lead \u8211\'20\'13 with a keen focus on the core PCE prices component. Other US data includes durable goods, jobless claims, Case-Shiller home prices, building permits and new home sales. The key event in Australia will be the release of Q3 CPI. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  has tried to balance battling inflation and maintaining reasonable growth by scaling back the size of the October cash rate hike to 25 basis points from 50. If the Aus CPI comes in hotter than expected, it could force the \uc2 RBA\uc2  to get more aggressive in coming policy meetings. Major data out of China \u8211\'20\'13 including Q3 GDP have been delayed and at this stage there hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been a date given for when they will finally be released. Japan data includes Tokyo CPI and employment data.\par \par \par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-27T04:52:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221024eiao00lu5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
