\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 18:28 GMT\par     EQUITIES\par     GLOBAL - U.S. and European shares rose on Monday as signs of a cooling U.S. economy\par raised hopes that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will slow its pace of rate hikes.\par     The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 346.66 points, or 1.12%, to 31,429.22,\par the S&P 500 gained 33.7 points, or 0.90%, to 3,786.45 and the Nasdaq Composite\par  added 37.59 points, or 0.35%, to 10,897.31 by 12:43 p.m. EDT (1643 GMT).\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     NEW YORK -  U.S. stock indexes climbed on Monday as shrinking business activity\par lifted hopes of a less aggressive approach by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 , even though the\par gains on the Nasdaq were capped by a slump in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesla\f28 \uc2 \f2  and some Chinese firms.\par     At 12:37 ET, the Dow Jones Industrial Average was up 366.01 points, or\par 1.18%, at 31,448.57, the S&P 500, was up 36.59 points, or 0.98%, at 3,789.34,\par and the Nasdaq Composite was up 45.59 points, or 0.42%, at 10,905.31.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     LONDON - European shares rose on Monday on hopes the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  could slow its\par pace of interest rate hikes, while investors welcomed Rishi Sunak's victory in\par Britain's prime ministerial race and looked ahead to a key rate decision from the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The continent-wide STOXX 600 index closed 1.4% up at its highest level in\par nearly a week, with utilities, media and travel and leisure\par sectors leading the gains.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     TOKYO - Japan's Nikkei share average gave up most of its morning gains during the\par afternoon session to close below the key 27,000 level on Monday, as investors fretted\par about the outlook for China's economy.\par     Japan's stock benchmark ended the day up 0.31% at 26,974.90. Of the\par benchmark's 225 components, 133 rose versus 88 that fell, with four flat.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     SHANGHAI - Hong Kong stocks slid to 13-year lows on Monday and onshore yuan fell to\par its weakest level in 15 years after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2 's newly unveiled leadership team\par heightened fears that economic growth will be sacrificed for ideology-driven policies. \par     China's bluechip CSI300 index lost 2.3%, while the Shanghai Composite\par Index lost 1.4%.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares closed firmer on Monday, led by gold miners and\par financials, as global investors took solace in hints that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par would debate on a smaller interest rate hike in December.\par    The S&P/ASX 200 index climbed 1.5% to 6,779.40 at the close of trade. The\par benchmark closed 0.8% lower on Friday. \par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     SEOUL - South Korean shares rose on Monday as investors found some relief in the\par government's measures to ease worries about a credit crunch in bond and short-term\par money markets. The Korean won strengthened, while the benchmark bond yield fell.\par     The benchmark KOSPI rose 23.04 points, or 1.04%, to 2,236.16 as of 0630 GMT\par at close. \par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     NEW YORK - The dollar was slightly higher on Monday following another suspected\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while sterling dithered after Britain's\par Conservative Party settled on its third leader this year and China's offshore yuan fell\par to a record low. \par     At 10:40 a.m. EDT (1440 GMT), the dollar was up 0.72% at 111.91 against a\par basket of six peer \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     SHANGHAI - Hong Kong stocks slid to 13-year lows on Monday and the onshore yuan\par fell to its weakest in nearly 15 years as global investors dumped Chinese assets after\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xi Jinping\f28 \uc2 \f2 's new leadership team raised fears growth will be sacrificed for\par ideology-driven policies. \par     Onshore yuan fell to its weakest level in nearly 15 years. Offshore yuan\par , in which trade began from 2011, slipped below 7.30 per dollar in late\par afternoon trading to a record low.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian and New Zealand dollars on Monday failed to sustain a\par recent rally, after traders, cheered by hope the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  might consider\par less aggressive hikes, shifted their focus back to underlying concerns about global\par growth.\par     The Aussie slid 0.6% to $0.6338, although it did hit 64 cents for the first\par time in more than two weeks earlier in the day. Support is at Friday's low of $0.6209.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     SEOUL - South Korean shares rose on Monday as investors found some relief in the\par government's measures to ease worries about a credit crunch in bond and short-term\par money markets. The Korean won strengthened, while the benchmark bond yield fell.\par     The won was quoted at 1,439.7 per dollar on the onshore settlement platform\par , 0.01% higher than its previous close at 1,439.8. \par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     TREASURIES\par     NEW YORK - \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields climbed on Monday as investors remained concerned\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would maintain its ultra hawkish stance on fighting inflation\par despite economic data pointing to a slowdown in U.S. business activity in October.\par     But they climbed back again, with the benchmark 10-year Treasury yields\par up at 4.269%, some 6 basis points higher than its Friday close, and two-year note\par yields at 4.515%, about 2 basis points higher than where it stood late last\par week.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     LONDON - Euro zone borrowing costs fell sharply on Monday as investors considered\par the possibility that central banks might slow the pace of monetary policy tightening\par next year. \par     Germany's 10-year government bond yield dropped 10 basis points (bps)\par to 2.337%. It hit its highest since August 2011 on Friday at 2.532% before the report\par on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Yields move inversely to prices.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     TOKYO - Japanese government bond yields rose to fresh multi-year highs on Monday,\par defying a climb in the futures market spurred by bets the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  may\par become less aggressive in tightening policy.\par     Benchmark 10-year JGB futures ended the day up 0.25 point at 147.85, but\par the cash 10-year note had yet to trade as of 0655 GMT. It last yielded\par 0.250%, the upper limit of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy band. (2JGBv1)\par     For a full report, click on\par     COMMODITIES\par     GOLD - Gold prices slipped on Monday, weighed down by a firm dollar and elevated\par U.S. bond yields, while expectations of another hefty rate hike by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par kept investors on the sidelines.\par     Spot gold fell 0.5% to $1,648.60 per ounce by 1:50 p.m. ET (1750 GMT). U.S.\par gold futures settled 0.1% lower at $1,654.10. \par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     IRON ORE - Singapore and Dalian iron ore futures edged up on Monday, after data\par showed top steel producer China's economy rebounded at a faster-than-expected pace in\par the third quarter, but concerns about COVID-19 curbs and property slump kept a lid on\par prices. \par     On China's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dalian Commodity Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the steelmaking ingredient's\par most-traded January contract gained 0.3% to 680.50 yuan ($93.83) a tonne.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     BASE METALS - Copper prices fell on Monday as investors worried about the pace of\par economic growth in top consumer China and the yuan fell to its weakest versus the\par dollar since 2008, making dollar-priced commodities costlier for Chinese buyers.\par     Benchmark copper on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) was down 0.6% at\par $7,579.50 a tonne at 1555 GMT.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     OIL - Oil prices steadied in choppy trade on Monday, as weakening U.S. business\par activity data eased expectations for more aggressive interest rate hikes, while data\par showing demand from China remained lacklustre in September limited prices.\par     Brent crude futures for December settlement fell 21 cents, or 0.2%, at\par $93.29 a barrel by 12:08 p.m. EDT (1608 GMT), after rising 2% last week. U.S. West\par Texas Intermediate crude for December delivery lost 34 cents, or 0.4%, to $84.71\par a barrel. Both benchmarks had fallen by $2 a barrel earlier in the session. \par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     PALM OIL - Malaysian palm oil futures ended the week 7% higher on Friday as the\par arrival of the monsoon season stoked production worries, although gains were capped by\par sluggish October exports. \par     The benchmark palm oil contract for January delivery on the Bursa Malaysia\par Derivatives Exchange rose 4 ringgit, or 0.1%, to 4,100 ringgit ($865.71) a tonne.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par     RUBBER - Japanese rubber futures sank to a near 1-month low on Monday as weaker\par automobile production amid global chip shortages fuelled fears about a demand slowdown,\par while sliding Shanghai futures also weighed on sentiment. \par     The Osaka Exchange rubber contract for March delivery, finished\par 1.3 yen, or 0.6%, lower at 219.9 yen ($1.5) per kg. It dove to the lowest since Sept.\par 26 at 219.4 yen earlier in the session.\par     For a full report, click on\par     - - - -\par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-24T19:35:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221024eiao03f2m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
