\par\par \b \uc2 Talk of Fed Rate Slowdown Offers Respite for Bonds and Stocks -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sam Goldfarb \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2022\par 19:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Signs that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might soon slow the pace of its interest-rate increases have helped calm Treasury yields for the moment, offering some hope to investors that a more-stable bond market could ease pressure on stocks.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, which move in the opposite direction of bond prices, largely declined Friday after The Wall Street Journal reported that Fed officials were poised to raise interest rates by 0.75 percentage point at their Nov. 1-2 meeting -- but were also likely set to debate shifting to a smaller increase in December. Yields were little changed in choppy trading Monday.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, investors began betting that the Fed would raise rates by three-quarters of a percentage point not just in November but in December. That was the latest escalation in interest-rate wagers that have pummeled bonds and stocks for months on end.\par \par \uc2 The idea that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might move by just 0.5 percentage point in December sparked an immediate rally Friday in shorter-term Treasurys in particular, given their sensitivity to the near-term interest-rate outlook. The surge in prices gained momentum later Friday, when \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  made comments that investors interpreted as signaling concern about raising rates too quickly.\par \par \uc2 Stocks joined bonds in their rally, with the S&P 500 notching a weekly gain of 4.7% and climbing another 0.7% in afternoon trading Monday. Investors enter this week preparing for earnings from tech companies including \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 ., along with data on economic growth and the Fed's preferred inflation gauge.\par \par \uc2 Taken together, recent events suggest that "the debate is shifting towards talking about how to get the market prepared for smaller rate hikes," said Thomas Simons, a money-market economist in the fixed-income Group at Jefferies.\par \par \uc2 Despite Friday's move, longer-term Treasury yields still ended last week significantly higher than seven days earlier, extending a historically bad year for bond returns that has had broad repercussions across financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note, which is especially influential in determining borrowing costs throughout the economy, climbed for a 12th straight week to finish at 4.212% -- up from 4.005% a week earlier. It was 4.219% in recent trading Monday.\par \par \uc2 Yields were fairly stable early last week. But without much of a catalyst, they climbed steeply on Wednesday and Thursday. That presented a setback for stocks, which are sensitive to rising yields because that increases the return investors earn risk-free by holding Treasurys to maturity.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said that Treasurys were still being pressured by the previous week's inflation data, which continued to show a rapid rise in consumer prices. Stubbornly high inflation has led to a significant readjustment in investors' expectations for short-term interest rates set by the Fed -- the trajectory of which plays a critical role in determining the level of Treasury yields.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, interest-rate derivatives for the first time this year showed a better than 50% chance that the Fed would raise its benchmark federal-funds rate above 5% in 2023, though that percentage was down to about 40% on Monday. The fed-funds rate is currently set at a range of 3% to 3.25% after starting the year near zero.\par \par \uc2 Investors also remain on edge about the potential for shrinking foreign demand for Treasurys, as global central banks aggressively raise interest rates, and in some cases, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Friday, momentum toward lower yields was partially offset by signs that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the yen for at least the second time in two months. The yen surged abruptly Friday morning a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had breached 150 yen per dollar, its weakest level in decades.\par \par \uc2 Some investors have worried that Japan's government is raising the dollars it needs to buy yen by selling some of its holdings in U.S. Treasurys. A possibly even larger fear is that the weakening yen will force the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to abandon its policy of holding down bond yields, creating a rush among Japanese financial institutions to sell large amounts of U.S. bonds in favor of their own government's debt.\par \par \uc2 "Is the concern that this is a risk to global financial stability warranted? I think yes," said \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2 , a senior fellow at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  and former adviser to the U.S. Trade Representative in the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's interest-rate policy "has contributed to the incentives that have led Japanese pension funds and insurers and other institutional investors to dramatically increase their holdings of foreign bonds over time," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Private Japanese investors have become among the biggest buyers of a range of U.S. bonds," but they could reduce their holdings if the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  shifts course, he added.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sam Goldfarb at sam.goldfarb@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 24, 2022 13:39 ET (17:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221024eiao0035x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
