\par\par \b \uc2 LIVE MARKETS-As yen approaches 150 again, can  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  stop its slide?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2022\par 18:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Main U.S. indexes advance; DJI out front, up ~1.2%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Healthcare leads S&P 500 sector gainers; cons disc sole loser\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Dollar up; crude, gold, bitcoin fall\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 U.S. 10-Year Treasury yield edges down to ~4.20%\par \par \uc2 Oct 24 - Welcome to the home for real-time coverage of markets brought to you by Reuters reporters. You can share your thoughts with us at\par \par \uc2 AS YEN APPROACHES 150 AGAIN, CAN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  STOP ITS SLIDE? (1231cEDT/1631 GMT)\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen weakened back to just below the key 150 level against the U.S. dollar on Monday, and some analysts think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair could still march higher despite what was suspected to be a record \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The yen strengthened from 151.94 to 144.50 on Friday, which analysts say was likely due to Japanese officials selling $30 billion or more U.S. dollars. It was back at 148.76 on Monday, after earlier reaching 145.28, which analysts said could have been due to an additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar also lost steam after the Wall Street Journal on Friday reported that some \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials want to slow the pace of rate hikes after its November 1-2 meeting, when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to hike rates by an additional 75 basis points.\par \par \uc2 But until a dovish pivot from the Fed becomes apparent, or the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  alters its extremely accommodative approach, the drivers pushing up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair are expected to continue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe BoJ meets to set monetary policy this Friday and unless we see a shift in its ultra-dovish outlook (a negative policy rate and ongoing quantitative easing) it seems hard to expect a top in USD/JPY anytime soon,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2 , Global head of markets and regional head of research for UK & CEE at ING said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Volatility in dollar/yen has risen from a seven-month low reached last Wednesday. It remains lower than in September, however, when the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Citi\uc2  analyst Osamu Takashima said that based on the reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of around $30 billion, the MOF spent more this time than in September for a weaker result and that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe see diminishing returns in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with an increasing spend needed to achieve the same \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The aggressiveness the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed in the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair at around 150-152 in the near-term, Takashima said.\par \par \uc2 However, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe don\u8217\'20\'19t think their action will change the JPY trend successfully as it lacks the backup from the BoJ,\u8221\'20\'1D Takashima said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair is likely to rise back above 152 in the medium term \u8220\'20\'1Cshould the overall USD strength continue under the pessimistic market sentiment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 (Karen Brettell)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 STOXX: UP BUT OFF HIGHS (1145 ET/1545 GMT)\par \par \uc2 News of \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  set to become the next UK Prime Minister, the appointment of a relatively pro-EU finance minister in Italy and steep fall in gas prices all made for a risk-on day across European equity markets, giving extra fuel to a rally sparked by hopes of a soft pivot at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But a disappointing \uc2 PMI\uc2  which offered the latest sign of a cooling U.S. economy ate into the rally, driving the STOXX 600 index off highs to rise around 1.4% at the close. The pan-European benchmark had earlier risen 2.1% to its highest in more than one month.\par \par \uc2 Here's your closing snapshot.\par \par \uc2 (Danilo Masoni)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 LICKING STICKY WAGE INFLATION NO EASY DANCE FOR FED, BOE (1130 ET/1530 GMT)\par \par \uc2 With U.S. and UK wage inflation running at a stubborn 5.5%, the Federal Reserve and Bank of England face a "delicate dance" of raising interest rates enough to lower that rate of gains without significantly boosting unemployment, says David Storm at \uc2 RBC Wealth Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BoE\uc2  is on a tightrope with its current rate hike trajectory as about two million UK households would see their mortgage costs double in the next 12 months, said Storm, the chief investment officer at the \uc2 RBC\uc2  unit.\par \par \uc2 Most of those mortgages could enter default as housing costs take up about 40% of wages in southeastern Britain, an undesirable outcome.\par \par \uc2 This "acts as a ceiling on just how high UK interest rates can go," he said. If such defaults were to occur, house prices could decline by 30%-40%, he said.\par \par \uc2 For the Fed, models point to an unemployment rate of 5-6% to deliver the required slowdown in inflation, and poses a risk in the U.S. market where record levels of equity holdings could be used to maintain current levels of spending.\par \par \uc2 RBC\uc2  expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  messaging to evolve and higher inflation will be tolerated later on, but short-term pain now to reduce inflation in the future is worth it, Storm says.\par \par \uc2 (Herbert Lash)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 CONTRACTION ACTION: FLASH PMI HEADS SOUTH (1045 EDT/1445 GMT)\par \par \uc2 U.S. business activity is in contraction this month.\par \par \uc2 S&P Global's preliminary "flash" purchasing managers index (\uc2 PMI\uc2 ) data for the manufacturing and services sectors landed at 49.9 and 46.6, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Both are now below 50, which signifies a monthly pull-back of activity, with a composite of the two drifting down to 47.3.\par \par \uc2 It was the manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2 's first contractive print since June 2020, when the economy was dragging itself from the wreckage of pandemic-driven lockdowns and the steepest economic downturn in history.\par \par \uc2 All three metrics came in below consensus estimates.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. economic downturn gathered significant momentum in October," writes Chris Williamson, chief business economist at S&P Global, adding that together, the surveys paint "a picture of the economy at increased risk of contracting in the fourth quarter at the same time that inflationary pressures remain stubbornly high."\par \par \uc2 The weak data provides some perverse encouragement, hinting at the possibility that the Fed's barrage of steep interest rate hikes are taking effect, tossing cold water on the economy and - hopefully - cooling down decades-hot inflation in the process.\par \par \uc2 "There are clearly signs that weakening demand is helping to moderate the overall rate of inflation, which should continue to fall in the coming months, especially if interest rates continue to rise," Williamson says.\par \par \uc2 This follows last week's data on Atlantic region manufacturing, which saw the Philly Fed and Empire State readings well below the contraction/expansion dividing line.\par \par \uc2 But things are tough all over.\par \par \uc2 Here's how the manufacturing data stacks up against the euro zone and China:\par \par \uc2 Wall Street was off to a mixed Monday, with the Nasdaq showing red, but the S&P 500 and blue-chip Dow clawing their way to modest gains.\par \par \uc2 Perennial market-movers \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  were weighing heaviest as investors favored value over growth.\par \par \uc2 (Stephen Culp)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 S&P 500 RALLIES EARLY, BUT RESISTANCE STILL RESOLUTE (1010 EDT/1410 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Wall Street's main indexes are mixed early on Monday, with an eye on Treasury yields. Investors are hoping for a less aggressive \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , as they brace for a busy week of earnings dominated by big technology companies.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, and S&P 500 are positive, while the is lower.\par \par \uc2 Defensive groups such as healthcare, and staples are among the leaders, while consumer discretionary is showing the most weakness.\par \par \uc2 That said, economically sensitive transports are also among stronger groups.\par \par \uc2 The NYSE FANG+TM index is taking a more than 4% hit. This given sharp declines in \uc2 Baidu\uc2  and \uc2 Alibaba\uc2  on Chinese stock weakness. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is also under pressure after slashing EV prices in China. TSLA is also the biggest drag on the consumer discretionary sector.\par \par \uc2 In any event, the SPX hit a high of 3,796.66 early in the session, and it has since backed away to around 3,760. Resistance at its 3,806.91 October 5 high, and at the 38.2% Fibonacci retracement of its March 2020-January 2022 advance at 3,815.20, is proving to be a tough barrier - click here:\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, after hitting a high of 4.3380% last week, the U.S. 10-Year Treasury yield fell as low as 4.1270% earlier on Monday. It is now up slightly on the day, at around 4.24%.\par \par \uc2 Here is where markets stood just after 1000 EDT:\par \par \uc2 (Terence Gabriel)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 CHINA LARGE-CAP ETF: BEAR HAS A TIGHT GRASP (0901 EDT/1301 GMT)\par \par \uc2 U.S.-listed shares of Chinese companies are dropping sharply in U.S. premarket trading after \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's newly unveiled leadership team heightened fears that economic growth will be sacrificed for ideologically-driven policies.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Hong Kong stocks slumped to 13-year lows; data also showed China's economy grew at faster-than-anticipated pace in Q3, but longer-term revival remained challenged by COVID-19 curbs and global recession risks.\par \par \uc2 In September, the \uc2 iShares China Large-Cap ETF\uc2  ended below a monthly support line from its 2004 lows:\par \par \uc2 The ETF has since seen downside follow-through in October. In fact, the FXI ended Friday off 34.6% year-to-date, putting it down more than 67% from its 2007 record close.\par \par \uc2 Now, ahead of Monday's open, the ETF is quoted down more than 8%, suggesting it is poised to fall to its lowest levels since November 2008 when regular trading kicks off.\par \par \uc2 Of note, in a testament to the tightness of the bear's grasp, momentum is fantastically weak. The 14-month RSI is at an all-time low.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, the FXI continues to severely underperform the S&P 500 index. The FXI/SPX ratio, on a monthly basis, is also at an all-time low.\par \par \uc2 The FXI's November 2008 low was at $20.90, while its October 2008 trough was at $19.35. Around its inception in 2004, the ETF traded as low as $16.73.\par \par \uc2 On the upside, the FXI faces the broken support line from 2004, which is now acting as resistance, around $28.55, as well as the down-trend line from its Feb 2021 high, now around $29.85.\par \par \uc2 (Terence Gabriel)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 FOR MONDAY'S LIVE MARKETS' POSTS PRIOR TO 0900 EDT/1300 GMT - CLICK HERE:\par \par \uc2 (Terence Gabriel is a Reuters market analyst. The views expressed are his own) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-24T17:31:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221024eiao034t5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
