\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-GRAPHIC-Take Five: Westminster woes, Tokyo on yen watch\b0\par\par\uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2022\par 09:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats Friday's story with updates to first and second entry)\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 24 (Reuters) - The breathtaking pace of political events in Britain is keeping traders on their toes, while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is back on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to deliver another bumper interest rate hike, and earnings season is ramping up with corporates in the United States and Europe facing very different cross currents.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at the week ahead in markets from Kevin Buckland in Tokyo, Lewis Krauskopf in New York, and Dhara Ranasinghe, Joice Alves and Karin Strohecker in London. Graphics by Vincent Flasseur and Sumanta Sen.\par \par \uc2 1/ WHO RULES BRITANNIA?\par \par \uc2 Brought down after just six weeks as British prime minister by a radical economic programme that rocked markets, pushed up living costs for voters and enraged much of her own party, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  has quit.\par \par \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  looked set to\par \par \uc2 become Britain's\par \par \uc2 next prime minister after \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  withdrew from the contest on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The Conservative Party, which holds a big majority in parliament and is not obliged to call an election for another two years, plans to select a new leader by Oct. 28.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Jeremy Hunt\uc2  is due to deliver a new budget on Oct. 31. He faces the uphill task of finding tens of billions of pounds in spending cuts - in the middle of a cost-of-living crisis - to rescue Britain's battered fiscal reputation, with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income markets in the doldrums.\par \par \uc2 2/ DETERMINED DOVE, HESITANT HAWK\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan meets on Oct. 28 and is expected to continue running against the grain of global central banks by sticking to its extraordinary levels of stimulus - even as its policy exacerbates a politically unpopular plunge in the yen to a 32-year low beyond 150 per dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday to buy yen for the second time in a month, and likely did so again on Monday. The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  conducted emergency bond buying last week to keep a lid on yields, showing its commitment to easing.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, the monetary policy outlook is much murkier. The Reserve Bank's decision in October to slow the pace of rate hikes reverberated in global markets, leading some to say the peak of policy tightening worldwide might be reached soon.\par \par \uc2 Recent data has backed the case for a slower pace of hikes with Australian employment numbers posting a disappointingly small rise in September. That put the spotlight on quarterly inflation data due on Wednesday that could help determine the next policy move.\par \par \uc2 3/ SUPER SIZE ME\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is widely expected to deliver its second supersized 75 basis-point rate hike on Thursday. Joining the global rate-hike party late, the \uc2 ECB\uc2  has delivered 125 bps worth of rises in two meetings in its fastest pace of policy tightening on record.\par \par \uc2 With euro area inflation at almost 10% versus an \uc2 ECB\uc2  target of 2%, there's little appetite to slow down now, even if recession risks are rising.\par \par \uc2 In fact, expect \uc2 ECB\uc2  chief \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  to face plenty of questions about when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will start scaling back its bondholdings in the next tightening phase. Policymakers are also believed to be closing in on a deal to change the rules governing loans to banks that have benefited from a sudden jump in rates.\par \par \uc2 4/ HERE COME THE BIGGIES\par \par \uc2 More than 300 S&P 500 companies are due to report results over the next two weeks as the U.S. third-quarter earnings season heats up. Reports from companies such as \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , Lockheed Martin, Bank of America and \uc2 Netflix\uc2  have heartened investors in those firms' shares.\par \par \uc2 Still, overall S&P 500 earnings growth is only expected to have climbed 3% year-on-year, according to Refinitiv I/B/E/S. That would be the worst performance in two years and is down from an expectation of 4.5% at the start of October.\par \par \uc2 Faltering profit growth would remove a key pillar that has helped shelter U.S. stock markets from more severe declines. Among companies due to report in the coming week are the four largest in the U.S. by market value: \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 5/ EUROPE INC. REALITY CHECK\par \par \uc2 It's a different story in Europe, where soaring costs of energy, raw materials, labour and credit and tangled supply chains are chipping away at earnings expectations.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, a different lockdown sequencing and favourable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  effects mean companies listed on the pan-European STOXX 600 regional index are expected to report a rise of about 28.4% in earnings in the third quarter, according to Refinitiv I/B/E/S. Nonetheless, that's down from an expected 32% jump pre-reporting season and a whopping 60% growth in Q3 2021.\par \par \uc2 So far results have been mixed: Telecom equipment makers \uc2 Nokia\uc2  and \uc2 Ericsson\uc2  missed analysts' expectations, but on a brighter note Birkin bag maker Hermes saw a sharp rise in sales growth, and the world's second-biggest spirits group \uc2 Pernod Ricard\uc2  beat expectations.\par \par \uc2 (Compiled by Karin Strohecker; Editing by Hugh Lawson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-24T08:18:54.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221024eiao00z15\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
