\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Will they or won't they? Japan uses guessing game to shore up yen\b0\par\par\uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2022\par 09:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Govt declines to confirm \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  except for Sept. 22 foray\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to be \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new norm - sources\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 MOF declines to comment on if it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Stealth action better than nothing but only buys time - analyst\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Japanese authorities are relying heavily on psychological tactics to fight yen bears, which means keeping markets guessing about their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than overt attempts to arrest the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline to multi-decade lows.\par \par \uc2 The battered yen has whipsawed in recent weeks, which analysts and traders attribute to government efforts to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a relentlessly strong dollar, amid worries about the negative economic impact of sharp yen declines.\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) confirmed its foray into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Sept. 22, it has not commented on other suspected instances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then, including a sharp move higher in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Masato Kanda on Monday declined to comment, when the yen jumped to 145.70 against the dollar from around 149.70 in early Asia trade in another suspected yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the strategy of staying mum keeps investors guessing on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thereby discouraging speculators from testing the yen's new lows.\par \par \uc2 That contrasts with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the 2011 earthquake and tsunami to quell sharp yen rises, in which authorities announced most \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "With stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , authorities can give markets the impression they could be stepping in more frequently than they actually have," said Atsushi Takeda, chief economist at Itochu Research Institute. "It's a psychological tool that can limit the frequency of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Unlike 2011, the government's more recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  require selling dollars - not yen - which is more difficult as it taps into Japan's limited foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 While its $1.3 trillion in foreign reserves is the world's second biggest, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  drained nearly 15% of funds readily available for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Sept. 22 action alone, making regular actions costly and unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 During the Asian financial crisis in 1997-1998, the last major series of yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government, Japanese authorities in most cases did not announce whether they had stepped in.\par \par \uc2 That means Tokyo will need to rely more on its words - or its silence - rather than reserves to shore up the yen.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to Reuters on Saturday, Kanda said that for the time being, the MOF, which oversees exchange-rate policy, would stick to its stance of holding off commenting on whether it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The MOF will probably maintain its approach of going stealth whenever it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a government official familiar with the matter. "It's hard to see why it would suddenly want to start announcing the fact it stepped in," the official said, a view echoed by another official.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  stuck to his script when approached by reporters on Monday, saying only that Japan will take "necessary" action against speculative yen moves.\par \par \uc2 WAR OF NERVES\par \par \uc2 Knowing solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone cannot reverse the dollar's broad uptrend, government officials have said any action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be aimed at slowing sharp yen falls rather than defending a certain level.\par \par \uc2 The MOF's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday came as the yen plunged to a fresh 32-year low of 151.94 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 By then, markets were rife with speculation Tokyo had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  including on Oct. 13, when the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped a full yen immediately after hitting a then 32-year low of 147.665 to the dollar. Another case was on Oct. 20, when the dollar fell 46 pips immediately after rising above 150 yen.\par \par \uc2 Facing public heat for rising inflation, Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  needs to show he is taking action to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall that has boosted import costs.\par \par \uc2 Engaging in a war of nerves with speculators is among the few options left for policymakers, particularly with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing no intention of hiking interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Investors will know how much the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  cost on Oct. 31, when the MOF is expected to release monthly data.\par \par \uc2 "The idea with stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to keep markets on edge, so it's important to be skilful and not allow traders to read your pattern," said Tsuyoshi Ueno, a senior economist at NLI Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 "Considering the recent steep rise in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, the speed of yen falls has been fairly moderate. You could say stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be better than nothing, though it's really just buying time." (Reporting by Leika Kihara and Tetsushi Kajimoto; Additional reporting by Yoshifumi Takemoto and Daniel Leussink; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-24T08:16:16.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221024eiao00yn9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
