\par\par \b \uc2 Up & Down Wall Street: The U.K. Is Worrisome. But Japan Looms Larger. -- Barron's\b0\par\par\uc2 1,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2022\par 03:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 6\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Randall W. Forsyth \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coincidence or coordination? You be the judge.\f28 \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the beleaguered yen. Around the same time, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  let it be known that it could slow the pace of its interest-rate increases late this year and into 2023.\par \par \uc2 While these actions by the two central banks are complementary, they fall far short of grand coordination efforts, such as the Plaza Accord of the 1980s. And their timing might be entirely coincidental. That is, if you believe in coincidences when it comes to the world's leading central bankers.\par \par \uc2 Much of the global financial world's attention has been diverted to the chaos in the United Kingdom, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets' vote of no confidence claimed \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  as their ultimate casualty. A new prime minister is slated to be selected this coming week and should fare better, lettuce pray. (A British tabloid bet that a head of that vegetable would outlast Truss' salad days as PM. As the Romans might say: Veggie vincit.)\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors piled on late on Friday by lowering its outlook for the U.K.'s Aa3 credit rating to negative from stable.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. debacle has garnered vastly more media play, at least in Western outlets, than the yen's steady and dramatic slide. But Japan's economy looms far larger, as the third-largest in the world, and more than half-again bigger than the U.K.'s. Indeed, California's economy tops Britain's.\par \par \uc2 The Land of the Rising Sun acted on Friday, finally, to support its sinking yen. At the turn of the year, it took a little over 115 yen to buy a dollar. By Friday morning in New York, the greenback was worth almost 152 yen, an effective devaluation of about 24%.\par \par \uc2 This has been a result of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's persistence in capping its 10-year bond yield at 0.25% by, in effect, printing yen ad infinitum to buy those bonds. At the same time, the Fed has sharply raised its federal-funds rate target, from near zero to a range of 3%-3.25%, and is also beginning to shrink its bond holdings. All of which has boosted the greenback's value, especially against the yen.\par \par \uc2 The yen's weakness, and the \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions to counter it, appear to have washed up on the shores of the U.S. market. To support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells dollars for yen. Those greenbacks are invested mainly in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  securities. The sale of the notes and bonds puts upward pressure on U.S. yields, especially given the worsening liquidity in the U.S. bond market.\par \par \uc2 That has exacerbated the main factor sending yields to multiyear highs -- the expectation that the Fed would continue to push its fed-funds target sharply higher to slow inflation.\par \par \uc2 As noted here a week ago, markets began to price in more-forceful rate increases following the worse-than-expected September consumer price report. Fed-funds futures had priced in a 75-basis-point hike for both the Nov. 1-2 and Dec. 13-14 Federal Open Market Committee meetings. And as of Thursday, the futures market was pricing in a further 50 basis points by next March, for a peak range of 5%-5.25%. (A basis point is 1/100th of a percentage point.)\par \par \uc2 Friday brought a downshift in expectations after The Wall Street Journal reported that, while the FOMC is on track for a 75-basis-point increase in November, it will debate whether to make a smaller move the following month. That made December's rate decision roughly a coin toss between a 50- and a 75-basis-point boost, and lowered the expected rate peak, in early 2023, to 4.75%-5%, according to the \uc2 CME\uc2  FedWatch website.\par \par \uc2 The effects were dramatic in other markets. Combined with the boost from the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen rallied 3%, to about 147 to the dollar, which would be equivalent to a 900-point surge in the Dow Jones Industrial Average. Treasury yields also eased, with the two-year note's dropping to 4.50% by late Friday from 4.63% in the morning, and the 10-year note's falling to 4.23% from 4.32% that morning -- the highest level seen since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Lower yields and higher bond prices were a tonic for stocks on Friday, which capped the best week for the major averages since late June. The Dow industrials notched their third straight winning week, adding 4.89% over the five-trading-day span. The Nasdaq Composite added 5.22% while the S&P 500 index was up 4.74%, as these two gauges scored their second weekly gains in the past three weeks.\par \par \uc2 While equities won big, some sectors of the capital markets remained under pressure. Greg Peters, co-chief investment officer at PGIM, the big asset-management arm of \uc2 Prudential Financial\uc2 , sees illiquid conditions among relatively shorter maturities of the bond market, notably corporates.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's rate-raising campaign has made money-market mutual funds an attractive place to stash cash once again with yields approaching 3%, as the Income Investing column points out. But, Peters notes in a telephone interview, that has siphoned off demand from what he calls "quasi-money funds," short-term bond funds that once offered a meaningful yield fillip over actual money funds.\par \par \uc2 Sales of short-term corporate bonds into an illiquid market have lifted these funds' yields to about 5%. Cash is still king, however, Peters contends -- even after a strong finish to the week for stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Write to Randall W. Forsyth at randall.forsyth@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 21, 2022 21:30 ET (01:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221022eiam0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
