v Carney - UK crisis shows risk of policies at cross purposes Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 06:00 GB Sep PSNB Ex Banks GBP 17.100 bln f'cast, 11.820 bln prev \u8226\'20\'22 06:00 GB Sep PSNB, GBP11.056 bln prev; PSNCR, GBP5.321 bln prev \u8226\'20\'22 06:00 GB Sep Retail Sales MM -0.5% f'cast, -1.6% prev; YY -5.0% f'cast, -5.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 08:30 GB Sep Consumer Credit, GBP 1.077 bln prev \u8226\'20\'22 08:30 GB Sep Mortgage Lending, GBP 6.136 bln prev; Approvals, 74.340k prev \u8226\'20\'22 14:00 EZ Consumer Confidence - Flash -30.0 f'cast, -28.8 prev Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 N/A BoE, Portugal CB Lisbon workshop (final day) \u8226\'20\'22 14:00 ECB Herodotou/Stournaras/Centeno in panel discussion (till Sat) \u8226\'20\'22 Sat ECB Lagarde at Frankfurt event Commentary and Analysis\par \par \uc2 Asia caution precluding USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , crosses mixed \u8226\'20\'22 Caution prevailed in Asia ahead of the weekend, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch on \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY in tight 150.06-29 EBS range, any spikes could elicit action \u8226\'20\'22 Been pattern this week for tight ranges in Asia, followed by moves up abroad \u8226\'20\'22 Slow USD/JPY grind up could continue post-Tokyo, 150.50 initial spec target \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese exporter, option-related offers trail up, importers on dips \u8226\'20\'22 So-so large $630 mln in option expiries today at 150.50 strike \u8226\'20\'22 Higher US yields to preclude any large retracement sans \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Tsy 10s @4.241%, 2s @4.609%, bias up on hawkish Fed, Gilt mkt relative calm? \u8226\'20\'22 Asia mostly risk off albeit mild, Nikkei -0.2% @26,951, E-Minis 3674, at par \u8226\'20\'22 JPY crosses mixed, EUR/JPY 146.68-96 EBS, relatively buoyant \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/JPY 168.08-79, below 170.08 high Monday, AUD/JPY 94.03-37 better bid EUR/USD-Offered tone as Cable weakness underpins USD \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened +0.15% at 0.9787 after a whippy US session \u8226\'20\'22 It slipped down to 0.9765 when GBP/USD led USD higher in early Asia \u8226\'20\'22 E-minis fell over 0.40% at the open to add to the USD bid tone \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market went quiet late morning and EUR/USD edged back up to 0.9780 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD has some support at 0.9765/70 where the 10 & 21-day MAs converge \u8226\'20\'22 Bids are tipped around 0.9700 with more support at the Oct 13 low at 0.9632 \u8226\'20\'22 Key resistance is at a down-trend line that descends to 0.9959 today \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD vulnerable while that trend-line contains rallies AUD/USD-Recovers from early dip in quiet Asian session \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened +0.21% at 0.6283 after retreating from 0.6356 when US yields rose \u8226\'20\'22 It came under pressure early Asian when E-minis opened -0.40% and Asian markets fell \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD traded down to 0.6261 before markets settled and AUD/USD based \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon it is back to trading around 0.6280 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is @ 21-day MA @ 0.6376 and break would suggest bottom in place \u8226\'20\'22 Bids are eyed ahead of 0.6200 with support at 2022 low at 0.6170 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD remains vulnerable while the 21-day MA contains rallies GBP/USD-Offered amid more dour data and political uncertainty \u8226\'20\'22 -0.25% near the base of a 1.1200-1.1238 range with solid interest on D3 \u8226\'20\'22 UK consumers at levels close to gloomiest on record - GfK \u8226\'20\'22 Next government, must convince voters that it can navigate turbulent times \u8226\'20\'22 Charts, daily momentum studies conflict, 21 day Bollinger bands flat line \u8226\'20\'22 Neutral 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages - as recent consolidation extends \u8226\'20\'22 1.1133 21 DMA and this week's 1.1440 high initial major support resistance \u8226\'20\'22 Major levels are 1.0925 October low and 1.1541 61.8% of the Aug-Sep fall \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling likely rangebound until UK political dust settles USD/JPY above 150, no action yet but caution prevails USD/JPY broke above 150.00 Thursday, reaching a fresh 32-year high of 150.29 on EBS in New York. The move elicited no actual \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, only ramped up jaw-boning in an effort to slow further gains. The lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not really surprising given broad U.S. dollar strength and another leg up in Treasury yields. With the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  remaining hawkish, U.S. yields looking set to climb further and Japanese monetary policy staying very easy, both Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are well aware of the futility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt JPY weakness. This does not imply \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Any USD/JPY spikes could trigger strong action with the official stance now focusing on excessive volatility and speculative moves. To wit, USD/JPY is up about 32% this year from its 113.47 January low. Anecdotal evidence suggests importer demand remains strong, especially on USD/JPY dips. Some Japanese investors too look to be collecting dollars on dips, along with speculative players. The bias for USD/JPY remains higher, and dealers see tests to the upside continuing. Resistance is likely from 150.50 with more at 151.00. August 1990 saw a high of 151.58, July 1990 152.23, June 1990 155.77, May 1990 159.60 and April 1990 a high of 160.35. All these levels will be targets for spec players along with any option barriers on the way. Sterling rangebound until UK political dust settles Sterling has traded in a choppy 1.0925-1.1493 range this month as political uncertainty swirled. Further consolidation appears likely in the short term until the next \uc2 UK government\uc2  is formed and announces its policy framework. Market reaction to the resignation of Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  after six turbulent weeks was muted. Thirty-year gilt yields fell three basis points to 3.950%, as \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields hit 14-year highs. GBP/USD closed up 0.1%, with EUR/GBP up 0.05%. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Position Tracker showed markets only modestly short sterling on Oct 17, so positioning, often a major driver of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, should not be a significant factor. BOEWATCH prices a 75 bps rate hike to 3.00% on Nov 3 at 93.45% despite \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  Deputy Governor \uc2 Ben Broadbent\uc2  expressing doubt that rates will rise as much as markets expect. A 50 bps hike would hit sterling. Former finance minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , considered a safe pair of hands, is the bookmakers' favourite to be the next PM. The contest could be decided on Monday. Whoever wins faces a daunting challenge, as cost-of-living pressures and energy prices surge, the economy heads towards recession, industrial unrest increases, and consumer confidence is near record lows. GBP/USD daily technical studies conflict as choppy consolidation extends. The major levels are 1.0925, the October low, and 1.1541, 61.8% of the August-September fall and the upper Bollinger band, which should contain moves. Hope for China stimulus may again prove fleeting Optimism is brewing again for more broad-based economic stimulus from China, buoying mainland stocks and keeping the yuan from new historical lows, but that may soon fade. After the benchmark loan prime rate was held steady on Thursday for a second month, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  said that it would "better implement" its prudent monetary policy to support growth and employment. That vaguely hints at more policy easing. Some analysts still predict another cut in banks' reserve requirement ratio before year-end. So far, easing has been mostly targeted and delivered piecemeal to select sectors, most recently real estate.Hope is emerging for a measured easing of China's zero-COVID stance. The brief but swift dips in USD/CNY and USD/CNH on Thursday revealed the market's eagerness to believe in that long-awaited policy shift. USD/CNY came off from nearly clocking a new 14-year peak, while the offshore yuan veered away from a record low of 7.2794. Until then, the dire state of China's economy is fuelling strong consensus bets against the yuan, fuelled by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's unrelenting hawkish tone. China's delayed economic data is backward-looking but could still have an impact on investor sentiment and thereby the yuan - along with the conclusion of the Communist Party Congress this Sunday. If the statistics bureau finally releases numbers that are expected to be weak, optimistic yuan dip-buyers may have their hopes dashed again.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-24T05:01:35.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221021eial00kwt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
