\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares subdued on rate-hike jitters, yen at 32-year lows\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2022\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 21 (Reuters) - Asian shares slipped on Friday as the prospect of aggressive interest rate hikes from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  soured investor sentiment, while yen wilted to fresh 32-year lows, with markets on high alert for any sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. benchmark 10-year Treasury yields scaled fresh 14-year peaks, weighing on the equities market and pinning down gold prices.\par \par \uc2 Sterling edged lower after a short-lived rally following the resignation of Britain's Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 European stock futures indicated stocks were set to decline, with Eurostoxx 50 futures down 0.92%, German DAX futures down 1.04% and FTSE futures down 0.47%.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was 0.64% lower but above the two-and-a-half year low it touched on Thursday. The index was set for second straight week of losses.\par \par \uc2 "It's all so tenuous... the problem is the macro environment still remains difficult," said \uc2 Shane Oliver\uc2 , chief economist at AMP Capital, adding that the market is in a tug of war between investors who see opportunities and those who are focused on the difficult environment.\par \par \uc2 Global markets have been extremely volatile as investors have worried that hefty rate hikes will push major economies into recessions before inflation is tamed, while an even stronger dollar as the Fed tightens could wreak havoc in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Philadelphia Federal Reserve President \uc2 Patrick Harker\uc2  on Thursday suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will "keep raising rates for a while," while U.S. economic data showed persistent labour market tightness.\par \par \uc2 As a result, 10-year Treasury yields continued its ascent and rose to as much as 4.2663%, its highest level since June 2008.\par \par \uc2 "It really is the U.S. bond show that drives broad markets and while liquidity is an issue, the talk is there are just no buyers," said Chris Weston, head of research at Pepperstone.\par \par \uc2 In Asia, Australia's resources-heavy share index lost 0.73%, while Japan's Nikkei slid below the key 27,000 mark on Friday.\par \par \uc2 China shares nudged higher as investors cautiously awaited policy signals from the ongoing Communist Party Congress, with the blue-chip CSI 300 Index up 0.2% and the Shanghai Composite Index gained 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , set to clinch a third five-year term as China's leader, will reveal the members of its elite Politburo Standing Committee at the conclusion of the twice-a-decade congress on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Japanese yen breached a fresh 32-year low, and last traded at 150.39 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened past the symbolic 150 level late on Thursday afternoon in Tokyo after being perilously close to the mark through the week.\par \par \uc2 It took a while, but yen is now above 150, ING economists said, adding that the fact that the yen crept across the line was enough to curb any reaction. "If so, onwards and upwards still seems the most likely path for the JPY to follow."\par \par \uc2 Fresh threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  made by Japanese policymakers have kept investors on alert, although there has been no news of further action since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's core consumer inflation rate accelerating to a fresh eight-year high of 3.0% in September, underscoring the dilemma the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  faces as it tries to underpin a weak economy by maintaining ultra-low interest rates, which in turn are fuelling an unwelcome slide in the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The only way \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  works is if it is backed up by change in monetary policy, AMP Capital's Oliver said. "Unless you get \uc2 BOJ\uc2  onside to tighten monetary policy at the same time as the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it's not going to make much of a difference."\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the pound was trading at $1.1192, down 0.36% on the day following Truss' resignation late on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index rose 0.124%, with the euro down 0.18% to $0.9765.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, gold prices were set for a second weekly decline, while oil prices were near flat.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee; Editing by Lincoln Feast and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-21T06:32:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221021eial00ouh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
