\par\par \b \uc2 EXPLAINER-Yen is past key 150 threshold. What's next?\b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2022\par 05:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 21 (Reuters) - The yen tumbled past the key psychological level of 150 to the dollar for the first time since 1990, defying Japanese policymakers' repeated threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Below are details on how Japanese policymakers could respond:\par \par \uc2 WHAT HAPPENED SINCE \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 'S LAST YEN-BUYING \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent roughly 2.8 trillion yen ($18.6 billion) in dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, when authorities acted in the markets to prop up the yen for the first time since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Since then, policymakers have repeatedly threatened to act against volatile yen moves. They also lobbied, successfully, to include in a G7 finance leaders' statement last week that authorities will closely monitor "recent volatility" in markets.\par \par \uc2 But the steps failed to prevent the dollar from gaining 7% against the yen since the Sept. 22 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While the yen has occasionally made suspicious blips, authorities have not disclosed whether they have stepped in since then.\par \par \uc2 WHAT COULD BE THE TRIGGER FOR NEXT \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Policymakers have repeatedly said they are looking at the speed of yen moves, not its level, in deciding whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The comments reflect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's need to respect a tacit agreement among G7 advanced nations that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be justified only when aimed at smoothing "excess volatility and disorderly moves" in the market.\par \par \uc2 That means Tokyo will avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a way that appears as if it is defending a certain yen level. Any future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thus more likely when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall picks up pace such as on Sept. 22, when it lost nearly a full yen within a short period of time.\par \par \uc2 WHERE IS THE PAIN POINT, NEXT LINE IN THE SAND?\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  has faced criticism by lawmakers for failing to slow the yen's relentless fall, which pushes up import costs and households' living expenses.\par \par \uc2 While authorities deny having a line-in-the-sand in mind, such political factors mean they do need to be mindful of defending psychologically important thresholds. They also look at technical charts for key support levels for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which, if broken, could accelerate its decline.\par \par \uc2 Some market players point to the dollar/yen's September 1990 high of 152.30 as the next threshold, then 155. Others say there is no major barrier until 160. A break above 160 brings the pair to levels unseen since the 1985 Plaza Accord, when major economies, including Japan, took coordinated action to reverse the dollar's uptrend.\par \par \uc2 WHAT COULD THE BOJ DO?\par \par \uc2 With the economic recovery still fragile, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has shown no appetite to tweak ultra-low interest rates or its dovish policy stance, which are blamed for pushing down the yen.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is struggling to maintain its yield curve control (YCC) policy, with rising global interest rates pushing the 10-year bond yield above its implicit 0.25% cap and putting upward pressure on the entire yield curve.\par \par \uc2 By forcefully controlling the 10-year yield around its 0% target with unlimited bond buying, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  is also giving speculators the green light in dumping the yen and contradicting government efforts to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp declines.\par \par \uc2 If public criticism over the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's policy heightens, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may come under pressure to modify its dovish communication on the future policy path. That could lead to an eventual tweak to YCC when the dovish governor, \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 , sees his term end in April, some analysts say.\par \par \uc2 So far, however, Kishida is defending the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's ultra-loose policy and Kuroda, who is facing calls among some opposition lawmakers to step down before the end of his term.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 150.2400 yen) (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-21T04:20:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221021eial00el1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
