\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID-Anarchy in the UK\b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2022\par 22:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Oct 21 (Reuters) - A look at the day ahead in Asian markets from Jamie McGeever Farewell risk-on rally, we barely knew you. Even British prime ministers last longer.\par \par \uc2 Investors on Thursday initially shrugged off \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 's resignation and the latest episode of British political pantomime, and Wall Street opened firmly in the green. But it couldn't defy gravity - or more specifically, soaring U.S. bond yields and rate expectations - for long.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields rose again to new multi-year highs - the 10-year yield has risen 12 weeks in a row, and is up 20 basis points this week alone - while the Fed's implied terminal rate hit 5.00% for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Incoming Q3 earnings reports would need to be block-rocking beats across the board to overcome monetary tightening on that scale, and unsurprisingly, they haven't been.\par \par \uc2 For Asian markets, the focus on Friday yet again will be firmly on Japan. The dollar has sailed to a new 32-year high above the psychologically important 150.00 yen level, and at the time of writing, Japanese authorities are nowhere to be seen.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bond market in the past 24 hours, an emergency bond-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  to get the 10-year yield back below the 0.25% cap. It just about managed it.\par \par \uc2 But there has been no direct action on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  beyond another volley of verbal warning shots. Note that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  the green light to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling almost $20 billion last month when dollar/yen was around 147.00.\par \par \uc2 Pressure on the yen and Japanese government bonds is mounting. If Japanese inflation figures on Friday come in hotter than expected, it will only intensify.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market knows Japan is sitting on around $1.3 trillion of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and is goading MOF/\uc2 BOJ\uc2  into spending some of it.\par \par \uc2 Key developments that could provide more direction to markets on Friday:\par \par \uc2 Japan consumer price inflation (September)\par \par \uc2 South Korea producer price inflation (September)\par \par \uc2 UK consumer confidence (October)\par \par \uc2 UK retail sales, mortgage loans, public finances (September)\par \par \uc2 Fed's Williams speaks\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jamie McGeever in Orlando, Fla.; Editing by Josie Kao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-20T21:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221020eiak0455i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
