\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Yen falls to a 1990 low against the dollar as the \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  once again  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  in the market\b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The yen has fallen below 150 units per dollar, a level not seen since August 1990. The weakness of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to increase despite the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's attempts to halt its fall, which led to an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market last September. So far this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 23% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accumulated a depreciation of 50% against the dollar since the highs reached in 2011. This weakness is driven by the rate divergence between the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , which is causing U.S. bonds to rise. Thus, the yield on the ten-year bond has reached 4.15%, while the yield on the Japanese bond at the same term stands at 0.25%.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan will meet next week to analyze the evolution of its monetary policy, although its officials have so far ruled out a rate hike to halt the weakening of the economy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced an emergency bond purchase of 250 billion yen (about 2.38 billion dollars) in two tranches, one for maturities between 10 and 20 years and the other for debt of between five and ten years. With these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is trying to limit the increase in the yield on its sovereign debt to 0.25%, as part of its stimulus package for the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has to choose between controlling the yield curve or fighting yen weakness," said Kristoffer Kjaer Lomholt, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 , in statements to \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 . The expert noted that "for now, the revealed preference continues to indicate a preference to keep control of the yen curve". La Fran\u231\'00\'E7aise AM comments that "history shows that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the yen does not stop its depreciation. In August 1998, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached almost 148 to the dollar even after the US authorities joined the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  in buying yen. Moreover, in a context of economic, monetary and geopolitical uncertainty, the dollar is prevailing."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221020eiak006nm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
