\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia stocks slip as risk appetite wanes on rate-hike fears\b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2022\par 07:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 20 (Reuters) - Asian shares fell on Thursday to their lowest since April 2020 as risk appetite among investors faded after inflation data across the globe re-ignited fears of aggressive interest rate hikes by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell as much as 1.8% to 436.14, before cutting some losses. The index was last down 0.79% at 440.82.\par \par \uc2 European stock futures indicated stocks were set to decline, with Eurostoxx 50 futures down 0.43%, German DAX futures down 0.45% and FTSE futures down 0.25%.\par \par \uc2 Japan's yen crept close to the psychological barrier of 150 per dollar after earlier marking a fresh 32-year low of 149.93, while the yield on the 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note touched a 14-year high, brushing off a weak housing report.\par \par \uc2 "Yields rose to fresh cycle highs and risk appetite soured," said Taylor Nugent, a markets economist at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  in Sydney. Wall Street snapped a two-day streak of gains on Wednesday, while the dollar bounced from two-week lows.\par \par \uc2 In Asia, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index was 1.12% lower, while Japan's Nikkei slipped 1% lower but spiked higher after \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported that China was considering cutting the duration of quarantine for inbound visitors.\par \par \uc2 China's stock market fell on Thursday while Hong Kong stocks hit levels last seen during the 2008-09 global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 China on Thursday kept its benchmark lending rates unchanged for a second straight month as authorities held off unleashing more monetary stimulus to avoid stark policy divergence with other major economies.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. dollar firmed as investors flocked to the safe haven after blistering hot inflation data across the world raised the prospect of central banks continuing with interest rate hikes even at the expense of growth.\par \par \uc2 Sterling fell 0.2% to $1.12005 even as the inflation data showed food prices have jumped the most since 1980. Britain's Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  has so far defied calls for her resignation, though markets are increasingly considering the possibility of her imminent replacement.\par \par \uc2 "It's worth considering whether a call for a General Election might actually be positive for sterling," ING economists said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan fell to a record low on Thursday. It bottomed at 7.2794 per dollar, the lowest level since such data first became available in 2011, and last traded 7.2615.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis\uc2  President \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2  said job market demand remains strong and underlying inflation pressures probably have not peaked yet.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to raise rates by 75 basis points for the fourth straight time at its November meeting.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Fed's "Beige Book" survey of economic activity showed that there was there was some easing in several districts, but firms noted price pressures remained elevated.\par \par \uc2 The rise in the dollar and yields pushed gold lower, with prices lingering at a three-week trough on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The fragile yen has been on a losing streak for 11 straight sessions as of Wednesday's close, and has renewed 32-year lows for the past six sessions.\par \par \uc2 "I think the risk of another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be very high," said \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  strategist Carol Kong. "It is a matter of time before they come into the market to support the yen."\par \par \uc2 Last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy yen for the first time since 1998, in an attempt to shore up the battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee in Singapore; Editing by Stephen Coates and Christopher Cushing) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-20T06:27:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221020eiak00p8d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
