\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia stocks slide as investors lose risk appetite, yields rise\b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2022\par 04:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 20 (Reuters) - Asian share markets fell on Thursday as investor fears over a looming recession crimped risk appetite, while Treasury yields rose on expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will remain aggressive in its interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Japan's yen crept close to the psychological barrier of 150 per dollar after earlier marking a fresh 32-year low of 149.93.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note touched a fresh 14-year high, brushing off a weak housing report. U.S. 10-year yields were last up at 4.139%, beyond the 4.136% high it touched earlier.\par \par \uc2 "Yields rose to fresh cycle highs and risk appetite soured," said Taylor Nugent, a markets economist at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  in Sydney, adding that hawkish commentary from central banks also weighed on sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street snapped a two-day streak of gains on Wednesday, while the dollar bounced from two-week lows.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell to more than two-year low of 436.0 and was down 1.6% at 437.16,\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index was 1.12% lower, while Japan's Nikkei opened 1% lower at 26,981.75 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 China's stock market opened 0.5% lower as the ruling Communist Party's twice-a-decade congress remains in session this week.\par \par \uc2 China on Thursday kept its benchmark lending rates unchanged for a second straight month as authorities held off unleashing more monetary stimulus to avoid stark policy divergence with other major economies.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. dollar firmed as investors flocked to the safe haven after inflation data across the world raised the prospect of central banks continuing with interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis\uc2  President \uc2 Neel Kashkari\uc2  said job market demand remains strong and underlying inflation pressures probably have not peaked yet.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to raise rates by 75 basis points for the fourth straight time at its November meeting.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Fed's "Beige Book" survey of economic activity showed that there was there was some easing in several districts, but firms noted price pressures remained elevated.\par \par \uc2 The rise in the dollar and yields pushed gold lower, with prices lingering at a three-week trough on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The fragile yen has been on a losing streak for 11 straight sessions as of Wednesday's close, and has renewed 32-year lows for the past six sessions.\par \par \uc2 "The ever-lurking threat of official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  perhaps slowing the pace we might otherwise have seen given higher global rates," \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 's Nugent said.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy yen for the first time since 1998, in an attempt to shore up the battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee in Singapore; Editing by Stephen Coates) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-20T03:01:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221020eiak00avq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
