\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Wall St ends red, Treasury yields climb on dour guidance and looming recession fears\b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2022\par 22:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates to market close)\par \par \uc2 By Stephen Culp\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Wall Street closed lower on Wednesday, marking the end of a multi-session rally, and Treasury yields spiked as gloomy data and downbeat corporate outlooks tossed cold water on investor risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 All three major U.S. stock indexes lost ground, while the benchmark Treasury yield shot up to touch a new 14-year high.\par \par \uc2 "It's partly a pause after the rally, some concern over higher-than-expected inflation in great Britain, and some companies expressing caution about the outlook going forward," said Peter Tuz, president of Chase Investment Counsel in Charlottesville, Virginia. "The market is taking a breather."\par \par \uc2 Market participants balanced a string of mixed company earnings, notably from \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 , \uc2 Travelers Companies Inc\uc2 , and \uc2 Baker Hughes Co\uc2 , against ongoing concerns over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate hikes to contain inflation could push the global economy into contraction.\par \par \uc2 "The market is still unsure as to when the Fed is going to recognize what they've done to date is beginning to take effect," said David Keator, partner at the Keator Group, a wealth management firm in Lenox, Massachusetts. "The Fed is taking its mandate of tackling inflation seriously, but there\u8217\'20\'19s been chatter of tightening too much."\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 99.99 points, or 0.33%, to 30,423.81, the S&P 500 lost 24.82 points, or 0.67%, to 3,695.16 and the Nasdaq Composite dropped 91.89 points, or 0.85%, to 10,680.51.\par \par \uc2 Data showing UK inflation hitting 10.1% in September pushed European stocks to break their recent winning streak.\par \par \uc2 The pan-European STOXX 600 index lost 0.53% and MSCI's gauge of stocks across the globe shed 0.89%.\par \par \uc2 Emerging market stocks lost 1.62%. MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan closed 1.65% lower, while Japan's Nikkei rose 0.37%.\par \par \uc2 A sell-off in \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds pushed the benchmark Treasury yield to its highest level since mid-2008 on expectations of continued aggressive interest rate hikes from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Benchmark 10-year note were last at 4.1272%, from 3.998% late on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond yield was 4.1259%, from 4.021% late on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rebounded from two-week lows as hotter-than-expected UK inflation data fueled recession worries, which dragged down the sterling and helped support the greenback against a basket of world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar index rose 0.7%, with the euro down 0.83% to $0.977.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also touched a 32-year peak against the yen, hovering close to a level that some believe could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen weakened 0.40% versus the greenback at 149.88 per dollar, while sterling was last trading at $1.122, down 0.87% on the day.\par \par \uc2 Crude prices edged higher on tighter supply conditions, bouncing back after hitting two week lows in the wake of U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's plans to release oil from strategic reserves.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude rose 3.30% to settle at $85.55 per barrel, while Brent settled at $92.41 per barrel, up 2.64% on the day.\par \par \uc2 Dollar strength weighed on gold, sending prices for the safe-haven metal to a three-week low.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold dropped 1.4% to $1,628.61 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Stephen Culp; Additional reporting by Dhara Ranasinghe in London; Editing by Nick Macfie, Chris Reese and Deepa Babington) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-19T21:24:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221019eiaj03y4p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
