\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Sterling dips as market trims \uc2 BoE\uc2  rate hike bets, yen scrabbles off 32-yr low\b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2022\par 14:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 18 (Reuters) - Sterling fell on Tuesday, after surging almost 2% the day before, as investors scaled back some of their expectations for \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  interest rates hike following Britain's dramatic U-turn on its fiscal plans.\par \par \uc2 The battered Japanese yen traded near a 32-year trough to the dollar at 149 yen, putting the major psychological barrier of 150 in focus.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, new British finance minister Jeremy Hunt scrapped most of Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 's economic plan and scaled back her energy support scheme. The historic policy U-turn aimed to stem a loss of investor confidence since Hunt's predecessor \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  announced on Sept. 23 a string of tax cuts with no details of how they would be paid for.\par \par \uc2 Market participants said that while the reversal helped shore up sentiment it also prompted investors to reassess the outlook for \uc2 BoE\uc2  interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "For the time being, the moves by the Chancellor will reduce the need for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to act as aggressively," said James Smith, developed markets economist at ING.\par \par \uc2 ING expects now a 75 basis-point rate hike in November rather a full-point move expected before the fiscal policy U-turn, Smith added.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BoE\uc2  said on Tuesday that it had not decided to delay again the start of its sales of government bonds, after the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  had previously reported that another postponement was likely.\par \par \uc2 Sterling, which rose after the FT report, fell 0.1% against the U.S. dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's denial to trade at $1.1333 at 1243 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has strengthened around 3% against the yen in October, hemmed in by trader nerves following the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998 on Sept. 22.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said after a recent \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  gathering that "there wasn't any discussion on what coordinated steps could be taken" about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 UK EFFECT\par \par \uc2 Investors expected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to remain volatile amid high political and economic risks facing Britain.\par \par \uc2 "Political developments are moving very fast in the UK... with these developments perhaps helping explain some of the swings in global risk assets," said \uc2 Shaun Osborne\uc2 , chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotiabank.\par \par \uc2 In early trade, improved investor sentiment after Britain's policy turnaround and a fall in energy prices lifted the euro and sent the safe haven U.S. dollar to an Oct. 6 low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro was volatile weakened against the dollar following German investor sentiment data, which albeit less pessimistic than expected, still painted a bleak picture of Europe's biggest economy.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last 0.1% higher at $0.98495 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index which measures its performance against six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including sterling, the euro and the yen - was last down 0.1% at 112.00.\par \par \uc2 A key European benchmark for gas price fell to its lowest level in four months.\par \par \uc2 "In addition to the now well stocked gas storage, the currently almost summer-like temperatures as well as the first long-term weather forecasts that predict a mild winter are likely to have caused optimism," said Thu Lan Nguyen \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Britain's policy U-turn saw the risk-sensitive New Zealand dollar surge more than 1%, already lifted by hotter-than-expected consumer inflation data, boosting bets for further rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie also got another boost after it got briefly propped up by minutes of the Reserve Bank's last meeting that showed the decision to slow the pace of rate hikes was "finely balanced."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy governor \uc2 Michele Bullock\uc2  reinforced that in a speech on on Tuesday, saying the \uc2 RBA\uc2  can keep pace with tightening by global peers.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie strengthened 0.7% to $0.6336.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Joice Alves. Editing by Mark Potter and Tomasz Janowski) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-18T13:54:08.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221018eiai02h4e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
