\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-European shares rise on UK U-turn, Wall Street set to open higher\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2022\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Graphic: Global asset performance {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2yaDPgn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Graphic: World \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds ZEW survey, Wall Street futures and \uc2 BoE\uc2  response to FT story)\par \par \uc2 By Elizabeth Howcroft\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 18 (Reuters) - European stock indexes rose on Tuesday and Wall Street was set to open higher, in a revival of risk appetite that analysts attributed to the turnaround in British fiscal policy.\par \par \uc2 Britain's new finance minister, Jeremy Hunt, on Monday scrapped Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 's economic plan, which had sapped investor confidence in Britain in recent weeks. Relief at the U-turn prompted a rally in Europe, which lasted through U.S. and Asian trading. Wall Street's gains were also driven by better-than-expected \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  earnings.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index hit a 12-day low during Asian trading hours, as investors became less risk-averse. Meanwhile, the yen hit a new 32-year low versus the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister repeated warnings that authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Market sentiment also benefited from a \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  report that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  was likely to delay the start of its sales of billions of pounds of government bonds. A spokesperson for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the report was "inaccurate".\par \par \uc2 At 0957 GMT, the MSCI word equity index, which tracks shares in 47 countries, was up 0.3% on the day.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's main European Index was up 0.9%, having touched its highest in 13 days. The STOXX 600 was up 0.4%, also down from a 13-day high it hit earlier in the session, while London's FTSE 100 was up 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were up as investors hoped for upbeat corporate earnings to counter the economic gloom. Nasdaq futures rose 1.7% while S&P 500 e-minis were up 1.4%.\par \par \uc2 Still, with high inflation and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening weighing on markets, analysts said the revival in sentiment could be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t say this a green light for a big rally," said \uc2 Antoine Lesne\uc2 , head of ETF strategy and research for EMEA at SPDR, adding that such moves would be more likely towards the end of the year if it looks like the end of the rate-hiking cycle is coming closer.\par \par \uc2 "The key positioning of investors for the moment is to remain very cautious," he said.\par \par \uc2 German investor sentiment rose slightly in October, to -59.2 from -61.9 in September, according to the ZEW economic sentiment index. But the index of current conditions fell, as respondents assess the economic situation as significantly worse than the previous month.\par \par \uc2 The British pound was down 0.7% on the day at $1.1277, having eased from Monday's 12-day high of $1.144.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index was 0.2% higher on the day, at 112.24 . The dollar-yen pair was steady at around 149.05 yen per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has gained about 3% against the yen in October, with the yen dropping sharply due to the gap between U.S. rate hikes and Japan's ultra-easy monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The euro was a touch lower at $0.9834.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Commission\uc2  was set to propose another set of emergency measures to tackle high energy prices.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone government bond yields rose, with the benchmark German 10-year yield up 7 basis points at 2.335%.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar was up 0.5%, having jumped after higher-than-expected inflation data spurred expectations that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  would raise interest rates by 75 bps at its policy meeting next month. The Australian dollar was steady.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Elizabeth Howcroft; Editing by Susan Fenton, Robert Birsel) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-18T11:29:04.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221018eiai01ri5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
