\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Pound bounces after new UK finance minister ditches most of 'mini-budget'\b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2022\par 13:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout, refreshes prices)\par \par \uc2 By Amanda Cooper\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 17 (Reuters) - The pound rallied on Monday after Britain's new finance minister ditched most of the government's multi-billion pound "mini-budget", while the Japanese yen touched new 32-year lows, with investors braced for any sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sterling rallied by as much as 1.4% after Jeremy Hunt, who Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  appointed on Friday, reversed large swathes of the 45-billion pound "mini-budget" that sparked unprecedented market turmoil in which the pound hit record lows and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hunt, who has served as health and culture minister under previous governments, replaced \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 , whose package of unfunded tax cuts on Sept. 23 unleashed a bond market sell-off.\par \par \uc2 Hunt said the country now needed to increase taxes and cut spending to rebuild stability and confidence.\par \par \uc2 Investors are keeping a close eye on \uc2 UK government\uc2  bonds, which rallied in price sharply on Monday, thereby injecting a degree of confidence into the broader market.\par \par \uc2 "The message is very much one of calm and getting a steady hand back on the tiller. The focus on market turmoil was interesting, being an acknowledgement that the UK and its fiscal policies do not exist in a vacuum, isolated from financial markets," Chris Beauchamp, chief market analyst at IG, said.\par \par \uc2 "This greater degree of self-awareness, as well as Hunt's reputation as being more of a 'safe pair of hands', certainly seems to have reassured everyone. For now the market seems happy to give the new chancellor time and space to put the government's house back in order."\par \par \uc2 The pound was last up 1.2% against the dollar at $1.13045 . It's regained almost 10% in value since hitting a record low of $1.0327 after the unveiling of the mini-budget.\par \par \uc2 Yields on the 30-year gilt were down 41 basis points at 4.37%, having veered above 5% at one point last week, forcing long-term investors such as pension funds to scramble for cash to protect their positions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the yen hit a new 32-year low of 148.895 against the dollar on Monday, raising the chances that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will again step in to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the yen for the first time since 1998, after the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  stuck to its policy of maintaining ultra-low interest rates, which has battered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities kept up their warnings to the market on Monday of a firm response to overly rapid yen declines, after last week's fall and meetings of global financial leaders that acknowledged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 "We are not making the argument that there is a clear line in the sand at 150.00 for Japanese authorities - the whole idea of a 'line in the sand' in the current \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market appears unrealistic - but it\u8217\'20\'19s likely that allowing a move above 150.00 may well trigger an acceleration of the JPY sell-off which is exactly what Japan is trying to avoid," ING strategist \uc2 Francesco Pesole\uc2  said in a daily note.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the dollar fell against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trading down 0.1% at 112.88. The euro rose 0.3% at $0.9749, while the Swiss franc fell 0.4% against the dollar to hold at 1.0013 francs, having hit parity on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Last week's red-hot U.S. inflation print has reinforced bets of another aggressive rate hike from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which in turn is boosting the dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Rae Wee; Editing by Muralikumar Anantharaman, Ana Nicolaci da Costa and Jan Harvey) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-17T12:51:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221017eiah025pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
