\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan keeps up warnings over rapid yen moves after G20\b0\par\par\uc2 517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2022\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yen hovers near 32-year lows vs dollar\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Japan authorities ready to respond to excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor reiterates need to continue easing policy\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Suzuki makes no comment on stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds finance minister's comment on 'stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ')\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 17 (Reuters) - Japanese authorities kept up their verbal warnings to the market on Monday of a firm response to any overly rapid yen declines, after a 32-year low for the yen last week and meetings of global financial leaders that acknowledged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Masato Kanda told reporters that "each country would respond appropriately" on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , following a \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  (G7) statement last week that indicated members would closely monitor recent volatility.\par \par \uc2 The statement stopped short, however, of giving signs of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2 , who after last week's meetings indicated \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not necessarily disclose when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, was quoted on Monday by the Nikkei business daily as saying authorities would take decisive steps against excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves driven by speculation.\par \par \uc2 "I won't comment about that," Suzuki told reporters later on Monday, when asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had conducted any stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since its Sept. 22 foray into the market.\par \par \uc2 The phrasing about "decisive steps" is at times deployed as a prelude to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although the authorities' warnings on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have not generally had consistent or long-lasting effects on the markets.\par \par \uc2 "We're constantly watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements with a sense of urgency," Suzuki was quoted as saying.\par \par \uc2 He reiterated his stance in comments to reporters later on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumped to a 32-year low near 149 yen late last week, stirring speculation about the prospects for stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month to prop up the yen, the first such effort since 1998, had quickly faded.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the yen was again hovering close to 149 per dollar, trading around 148.80.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities are concerned that the yen's sudden and sharp declines, spurred by interest rate hikes in the United States and elsewhere while Japan sticks to an ultra-easy monetary policy, will further boost already high import costs that are squeezing households and companies.\par \par \uc2 The yen's sharp moves also heighten uncertainty for firms in making business decisions.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers have said they won't seek to defend a certain yen level, and instead will focus on smoothing volatility.\par \par \uc2 Kanda said that participants in last week's meetings also agreed that their central banks must calibrate the pace of monetary policy tightening, taking into account cross-border spillover effects.\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  reiterated on Monday, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue easing policy given that core consumer inflation would peak later in the fiscal year to next March and fall below 2% in fiscal year 2023, as cost-push inflation runs its course. (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Kim Coghill, Sam Holmes and Edmund Klamann) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-17T12:00:43.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221017eiah001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
