\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-G7 fails to reach  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  deal to ease pain of soaring dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 1,039 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2022\par 00:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 G7 says closely monitoring 'recent volatility' in markets\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Japan's jawboning fails to stop yen's fall to 32-year lows\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yellen shuns joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  similar to 'Plaza Accord'\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 South Korea hikes rates as dollar pushes up import costs\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Onus is on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support yen via solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Oct 16 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other countries facing the fallout from a soaring U.S. dollar found little comfort from last week's meetings of global finance officials, with no sign that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  along the lines of the 1985 "Plaza Accord" was on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 With a strong push from Japan, finance leaders of the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  advanced economies included a phrase in a statement on Wednesday saying they will closely monitor "recent volatility" in markets.\par \par \uc2 But the warning, as well as Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2 's threat of another yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , failed to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from sliding to fresh 32-year lows against the dollar as the week came to a close.\par \par \uc2 While Suzuki may have found allies grumbling over the fallout from the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive interest rate hike path, he conceded that no plan for a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the works.\par \par \uc2 "Many countries saw the need for vigilance to the spill-over effect of global monetary tightening, and mentioned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in that context. But there wasn't any discussion on what coordinated steps could be taken," Suzuki said in a news conference on Thursday after attending separate meetings of the G7 and G20 finance leaders in Washington.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  made clear that Washington had no appetite for concerted action, saying the dollar's overall strength was a "natural result of different paces of monetary tightening in the United States and other countries."\par \par \uc2 "I've said on many occasions that I think a market-determined value for the dollar is in America's interest. And I continue to feel that way," she said on Tuesday, when asked if she would consider a Plaza Accord 2.0 agreement.\par \par \uc2 NO YEN SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 In 1985, a destabilizing surge in the dollar prompted five countries - France, Japan, the United Kingdom, the United States and what was then West Germany - to band together to weaken the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and help reduce the U.S. trade deficit. Following the deal, named the Plaza Accord for the famed New York hotel where it was hammered out, the dollar shed roughly 25% of its value over the ensuing 12 months.\par \par \uc2 With no current U.S. interest in engineering that kind of deal, other countries have to find ways to mitigate the pain stemming from a strong dollar, which has forced some emerging economies to hike interest rates to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  even at the cost of cooling economic growth more than they want.\par \par \uc2 Emerging Asian nations have seen significant capital outflows this year that are comparable to previous stress episodes, heightening the need for policymakers to build liquidity buffers and take other steps to prepare for turbulence, said Sanjaya Panth, deputy director for the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's Asia and Pacific Department.\par \par \uc2 "The situation for Asian economies is very different from where they were 20 years ago" as countries accumulated foreign reserves that make them more resilient to external shocks, Panth told Reuters on Thursday on the sidelines of the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  annual meetings in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "At the same time, the rising debt levels, particularly in some economies in the regions, are a concern," he said. "Some form of market stress cannot be ruled out."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  delivered its second-ever 50-basis-point interest rate hike on Wednesday and made clear the won's 6.5% slide against the dollar in September that drove up import costs played a key role in the decision.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Rhee Chang-yong said on Saturday he does not sense an interest among U.S. officials to stem the dollar's strength through joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he said some kind of international cooperation on the dollar may be needed "after a certain period."\par \par \uc2 "I think a too-strong dollar, especially for a substantial period, won't be good for the Unites States either, and actually I'm thinking about the long-term implication for the trade deficit, and maybe another global imbalance may happen," he said.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, the onus is on the government to deal with a renewed plunge in the yen, caused in part by the policy divergence between the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's determination to raise U.S. interest rates and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's resolve to keep borrowing costs ultra-low.\par \par \uc2 At the news conference where Suzuki issued his warning about sharp yen falls, \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  ruled out anew the chance of a rate hike.\par \par \uc2 The dollar jumped about 1% to a fresh 32-year high of 148.86 yen on Friday, testing authorities' resolve to combat the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's relentless slide. The dollar/yen is now up roughly 2% from levels when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 22 to buy yen for the first time since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers have said they won't seek to defend a certain yen level, and instead will focus on smoothing volatility.\par \par \uc2 Masato Kanda, the country's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, told reporters on Friday that authorities were ready to take "decisive action any time" if excessively volatile yen moves continued.\par \par \uc2 Even moderating abrupt yen moves, however, could be a challenge as Kuroda's assurance that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  will keep interest rates in negative territory gives investors a green light to continue dumping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's impossible to reverse the yen's downtrend with solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Daisaku Ueno, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Once the yen falls below 150 to the dollar, it's hard to predict where its depreciation could stop because there's no technical chart support until around 160," he said. (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Additional reporting by Daniel Burns in Washington and Tetsushi Kajimoto in Tokyo; Editing by Paul Simao)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-19T22:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221016eiag0174t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
