\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar edges higher, pushing yen to new 32-year low\b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2022\par 13:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON/SINGAPORE Oct 14 (Reuters) - The dollar edged higher on Friday after dropping the previous day despite U.S. inflation accelerating, helping it hit a 32-year peak against Japan's yen.\par \par \uc2 Sterling slipped after a sharp rally on Thursday, as reports said British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  was preparing to sack her finance minister and carry out a major U-turn on the government's tax plans.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index was last up 0.35% to 112.97, having fallen 0.6% on Thursday as investors seemingly brushed off data that showed U.S. consumer prices increased more than expected in September.\par \par \uc2 The greenback has been on a tear this year as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has ramped up interest rates in an effort to tame inflation, pulling money back towards the United States. Fears about the global economy have also boosted the safe haven asset.\par \par \uc2 Yet, the stronger-than-expected inflation data on Thursday counter-intuitively triggered a rally in global stock markets and a fall in the dollar. Analysts suggested short-sellers reversing their positions seemed to have driven the bounce in equities, weighing on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the detail perhaps wasn't as worrying as the particular core (inflation) print suggested, so when the market started to sell off, people began to cover very quickly," said James Malcolm, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Japan's battered yen remained under pressure despite the brighter global mood.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to a new 32-year high of 147.785 yen on Friday, and was last up 0.34% to 147.705.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen for the first time since 1998. Investors remained on watch for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after finance minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  on Thursday reiterated the government's readiness to take action against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 Masayuki Kichikawa, chief macro strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui Asset Management, said he thought the yen could still hit 150 per dollar in the near future.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is targeting any specific level or line in the sand," he said. "What they are saying is they are trying to prevent excessive volatility."\par \par \uc2 The British pound slipped on Friday, after rallying 2.1% on Thursday on reports the government could cancel many of its plans for unfunded tax cuts. Sterling was last down 0.77% to $1.1244.\par \par \uc2 The Times reported that Truss planned to fire Finance Minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 , whose "mini" budget last month triggered chaos in markets and who cut short his trip to the United States to return to London on Friday. Truss was scheduled to hold a press conference later on Friday.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2 's Malcolm said the biggest impact from any U-turn would likely be felt in government bond markets, and backed the pound to recover next year after slumping to a record low last month.\par \par \uc2 "For the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market it's a little bit of a distraction," he said. "I would be very constructive on a 12 month basis on the pound, and only a little bit negative or cautious through the end of the year."\par \par \uc2 Focus now shifts to next month's Fed meeting where it is expected to deliver a fourth consecutive 75-basis-point rate increase. Traders were also waiting for U.S. retail sales data due at 1230 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was up 0.1% versus the greenback at $0.6301, coming off a two-and-a-half year low it touched in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Harry Robertson in London, Ankur Banerjee in Singapore, Tom Westbrook in Sydney and Kevin Buckland in Tokyo; Editing by Jacqueline Wong and Mark Potter) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-14T12:24:37.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221014eiae01w7m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
