\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Falls to 32-Year Low After U.S. Inflation Data -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megumi Fujikawa \par \uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2022\par 14:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The yen hit a 32-year low against the dollar on Friday after U.S. inflation data ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/us-inflation-september-2022-consumer-price-index-11665628037"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.wsj.com/articles/us-inflation-september-2022-consumer-price-index-11665628037}}}\uc2 ) suggested the interest-rate difference between the U.S. and Japan was likely to widen.\par \par \uc2 The yen traded Friday at 147.70 to the dollar, the lowest level since August 1990, compared with around 146.70 to the dollar before the inflation release Thursday. The U.S. data showed core inflation continuing to rise, fueling anticipation of further large rate increases by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Interest-rate differentials between the U.S. and Japan--where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has committed to keeping rates near zero--have been the major driver of the yen's fall this year. While most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have fallen against the dollar, the yen's drop has been among the biggest.\par \par \uc2 After a meeting of the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  finance ministers in Washington, D.C., Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  on Thursday reiterated his readiness to take action to stop the yen from falling rapidly. "We will take appropriate action if there are any excessive moves," he said.\par \par \uc2 Sitting beside Mr. Suzuki, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  said he was not considering raising interest rates because he believed inflation in Japan, currently running around 3%, would fall below the bank's 2% target in the year starting April 2023.\par \par \uc2 The government said Sept. 22 that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/japan-intervenes-in-currency-market-to-support-yen-11663836942"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.wsj.com/articles/\b  \highlight22japan\b0 \highlight22 -\b  \highlight22intervenes\b0 \highlight22 -in-\b  \highlight22currency\b0 \highlight22 -market-to-support-yen-11663836942}}}\uc2 ) to prop up the yen. It hasn't disclosed any \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then.\par \par \uc2 NOTE: In-line links reference additional content of interest chosen by the WSJ news team.\par \par \uc2 This item is part of a Wall Street Journal live coverage event. The full stream can be found by searching P/WSJL (WSJ Live Coverage).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 14, 2022 08:07 ET (12:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221014eiae001r8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
