\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rally pauses for breath after global stocks rebound\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2022\par 10:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE/LONDON Oct 14 (Reuters) - The dollar was little changed on Friday after investors flocked back to stocks despite the previous day's red-hot U.S. inflation data, while traders remained on edge about the prospect of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen.\par \par \uc2 Sterling slipped, after rallying sharply on Thursday on reports that the \uc2 British government\uc2  may soon cancel major parts of its highly contentious fiscal plans.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index was last up very slightly at 112.62, having fallen 0.6% on Thursday as investors seemingly brushed off data that showed U.S. consumer prices increased more than expected in September.\par \par \uc2 The greenback has been on a tear this year as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has ramped up interest rates in an effort to tame inflation, pulling money back towards the United States. Fears about the global economy have also boosted the safe haven asset.\par \par \uc2 Yet, the stronger-than-expected inflation data on Thursday counterintuitively triggered a rally in global stock markets and a fall in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Short-sellers in the stock market seemed to be driving the bounce in equities, which in turn pushed the dollar lower, said \uc2 Bank of Singapore\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Moh Siong Sim.\par \par \uc2 "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is taking its cue from the equity market," he said.\par \par \uc2 But Japan's battered yen remained under pressure despite the brighter global mood.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last up 0.21% at 147.53 yen, a whisker away from Thursday's 32-year high of 147.67.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen for the first time since 1998. Investors remained on watch for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after finance minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  on Thursday reiterated the government's readiness to take action against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 Masayuki Kichikawa, chief macro strategist at Sumitomo Mitsui Asset Management, said he thought the yen could still hit 150 per dollar in the near future.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is targeting any specific level or line in the sand," he said. "What they are saying is they are trying to prevent excessive volatility."\par \par \uc2 The British pound fell in early London trading as investors waited to see whether the Truss government would scrap many of the unfunded tax cuts which have wreaked havoc in markets. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2 's emergency bond-buying scheme was also scheduled to end on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Sterling made steep gains on Thursday but was last down 0.27% at $1.1301, with UK minister Greg Hands saying there were no plans to change anything in last month's "mini" budget.\par \par \uc2 Yet, British finance minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  cut short his trip to the United States, sparking speculation a U-turn was imminent.\par \par \uc2 Focus now shifts to next month's Fed meeting where it is expected to deliver a fourth consecutive 75-basis-point rate increase. Traders were also waiting for U.S. retail sales data due at 1230 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was up 0.42% versus the greenback at $0.6322, coming off a two-and-a-half year low it touched in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee in Singapore, Tom Westbrook in Sydney, Kevin Buckland in Tokyo, and Harry Robertson in