\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-\uc2 IMF\uc2  sees  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\uc2  as 'signaling' move with short-term impact\b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2022\par 02:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 IMF\uc2  official: Not the time for \uc2 BOJ\uc2  yield control tweaking\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely tied to yen's impact on capex\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was one-time event of relatively small size\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't last long, \uc2 IMF\uc2  says\par \par \uc2 (Adds additional \uc2 IMF\uc2  comment, finance minister's quote)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Oct 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month to stop a sharp slide in the yen was likely a "signaling action" to smooth volatility, though the impact of such moves tend to be short-lived, a senior \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  official said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Recent volatile market moves heighten the need for the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to maintain ultra-low interest rates and avoid making tweaks to yield curve control (YCC), Sanjaya Panth, deputy director for the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's Asia and Pacific Department, told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "We think this is not a good time to change YCC. In a particularly volatile situation where markets are edgy and many things are happening, you want to offer continued commitment to monetary easing until inflation picks up durably," Panth said.\par \par \uc2 "It's a very important signal the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  needs to provide. Tinkering with that right now may confuse markets. We don't see room for that," he said.\par \par \uc2 Some investors speculate the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  could tweak YCC and allow Japanese yields to rise more to moderate the pace of yen falls.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent roughly 2.8 trillion yen ($19 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last month to arrest sharp drops in the value of the yen, which were driven largely by the policy divergence between the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive interest rate hikes and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's resolve to keep monetary policy ultra-loose.\par \par \uc2 Markets are focusing on whether Japan will step in again, as stronger-than-expected U.S. inflation data pushed the dollar to a fresh 32-year high against the yen of 147.665.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in Washington, Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said on Thursday authorities were ready to take "appropriate action" against excessive volatility.\par \par \uc2 Panth said Japanese authorities likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month with the view that the yen's "pretty sharp" moves could dampen corporate investment and hurt consumer confidence.\par \par \uc2 Although interest rate increases by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain key drivers of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, authorities most likely saw the yen's recent "particularly sharp moves" as driven by no new information of relevance, he said.\par \par \uc2 "It was a fairly small amount given how liquid the market is," Panth said, referring to the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It seems more of a signaling action to smooth the market's adjustment."\par \par \uc2 "When there is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it does slow down the pace of depreciation. We saw that in this round in September. When looked at historically, the impact of these kinds of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't last very long," he said.\par \par \uc2 Once welcomed as giving exports a boost, the weak yen has become a headache for Japanese policymakers by inflating the cost of importing already expensive fuel and food.\par \par \uc2 "What is of relevance is the overall stance of monetary and fiscal policy, which remains appropriate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a one-time event so far of relatively small magnitude in a deep market."\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 147.0500 yen) (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Paul Simao and Gerry Doyle) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-14T01:39:23.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221014eiae002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
