oves in line with moves in US rates, Treasury 10s steady, @3.924%\par \u8226\'20\'22 Option expiries, possible barriers help bracket market\par \u8226\'20\'22 Expiries today 146.00/146.25 $926 mln, 147.25 $1.9 bln, tom 147.00 $840 mln\par \u8226\'20\'22 Talk too of possible barriers at 147.00, every big figure to 150.00\par \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese exporter offers pre-147.00 too, above, importer demand on dips\par \u8226\'20\'22 Asia risk off, Nikkei -0.5% @26,260, larger declines elsewhere\par \u8226\'20\'22 JPY crosses steady, EUR/JPY 142.37-64 EBS, AUD/JPY 91.96-92.35\par \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/JPY 162.63-163.17, GBP likely volatile till \f28 \uc2 \f2 BoE\f28 \uc2 \f2  deadline Friday\par EUR/USD-Hovers around 0.9700 as market eyes US CPI for direction\par \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened 0.9700/10 for 3rd straight day after recovering from 0.9668\par \u8226\'20\'22 It traded to 0.9722 early when USD broadly eased and E-minis opened higher\par \u8226\'20\'22 It then drifted down to 0.9695 before settling just above 0.9700\par \u8226\'20\'22 Consolidation will likely end with the release of the US CPI later today\par \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is @ 10-day MA @ 0.9807 with key resistance at trendline @ 1.0010\par \u8226\'20\'22 Bids are tipped at 0.9660/70 with break below targeting trend low at 0.9528\par \u8226\'20\'22 German CPI will be released before the US CPI is out\par AUD/USD-Consolidates below 0.6300 in quiet trading ahead of US CPI\par \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened 0.6278 after recovering from 2-year low at 0.6235\par \u8226\'20\'22 It traded up to 0.6290 early Asia before drifting lower later in the morning\par \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon session it is trading around 0.6270\par \u8226\'20\'22 A 1.25% fall in Dalian iron ore and 0.55% loss in AXJ equity index weighed\par \u8226\'20\'22 Trading was subdued ahead of key US CPI later today\par \u8226\'20\'22 A hot US CPI would likely send AUD/USD towards 76.4 of 2020 move at 0.6099\par \u8226\'20\'22 Support is at yesterday's and 2-year low at 0.6235\par \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD trending lower with the 5, 10 & 21-day MAs in a bearish alignment\par \u8226\'20\'22 A break above the 10-day MA at 0.6381 is needed to relieve pressure\par GBP/USD-Softer - focus on UK debt markets and U.S. CPI\par \u8226\'20\'22 Off 0.15% at the base of an active 1.1079-1.1120 range on D3\par \u8226\'20\'22 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  majors little changed in tight ranges in Asia ahead of U.S. CPI\par \u8226\'20\'22 UK house price growth slows, as mortgage costs surge\par \u8226\'20\'22 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  expected to raise rates by 75bp/100bp on Nov 3rd\par \u8226\'20\'22 30yr gilt up 9bp to 4.892% Wednesday, after October's 3.842% opening\par \u8226\'20\'22 If house prices remain resilient in this environment, they will never fall\par \u8226\'20\'22 Charts, positive daily momentum studies, 21 day Bollinger bands edge lower\par \u8226\'20\'22 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages crest or fall - daily signals are net bearish\par \u8226\'20\'22 NY 1.1025 low and Asia's 1.1120 high are initial support and resistance\par USD/JPY all about yields, possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par USD/JPY remains all about yields and possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The bias will stay higher on\par diverging Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Federal Reserve monetary policies and widening Japan-U.S. interest\par rate differentials, but upside moves are likely to be tempered by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on any rapid yen\par declines. U.S. yields remain very bid on hawkish Fed expectations - in contrast with a \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par adamantly sticking to its ultra-easy monetary policy. Policy will likely remain easy until\par Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2 's term expires in April 2023. With the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2  capping long-term interest\par rates under yield curve control (a cap of around 0.25% remains in place for 10-year JGBs),\par Japan-U.S. interest rate differentials have widened considerably. For 10-year maturities, the\par gap widened to around 376 basis points in September. In twos, the gap is around 441 bps. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem JPY declines remains a threat following action on Sept 22 but Japanese\par officials have since made it clear that they are more fearful of the speed of JPY weakness than\par weakness itself, which is inevitable given diverging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stances. Any real action will\par thus depend on the speed of any further falls. The bias among Tokyo players is for further\par USD/JPY gains and a test of 150 is expected, if not this year then into 2023. Japanese exporters\par will continue to sell into rallies and importers and investors will continue to buy on dips.\par Defence of topside option barriers is also likely, especially at the 150 strike.\par Softer U.S. CPI may give EUR/USD bears chance to sell\par EUR/USD has been consolidating this week, but expect volatility when U.S. CPI is released later\par on Thursday. If inflation undershoots forecasts, short EUR/USD positions will likely be\par squeezed, presenting a selling opportunity for longer-term bears. The market is expecting\par September's U.S. CPI to rise 0.2% month-on-month and 8.1% annually following increases of 0.1%\par and 8.3% in August. A forecast-beating outcome will likely see EUR/USD break below 0.9650 and\par head for its 2022 and 20-year low at 0.9528. If the data comes in softer than expected,\par short-covering will likely take EUR/USD above the 21-day moving average at 0.9807. Yet unless\par there is a sizable shift in \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  expectations, the gains will likely be\par short-lived. The euro zone continues to face severe economic headwinds from the war in Ukraine,\par data suggesting stagflation, and a trade shock resulting from slowing global growth. A slight\par downside miss on U.S. CPI for one month is unlikely to change the bearish outlook for the single\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Selling EUR/USD on a break below 0.9650 or on rallies above 0.9810 is the favoured\par strategy in the wake of the U.S. CPI. There is a downtrend line from February that comes in at\par 1.0010 and shorts should place a stop above that significant resistance.\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 To read our latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news and analysis services, click on\f28 \par \par \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-16T05:14:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221013eiad00jg1\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2